  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-REF-015$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$How does an employee display emotional presence when unexpected technical issues interrupt a live client presentation?$qb$, $qb$Preserving composure, acknowledging the interruption calmly, and transitioning smoothly to offline summary materials.$qb$, $qb$Apologizing repeatedly to stakeholders while attempting to resolve technical issues on screen.$qb$, $qb$Requesting a colleague to take over verbal presentation duties while you troubleshoot software issues.$qb$, $qb$Proposing a temporary meeting pause so participants can reconvene after technical issues are fixed.$qb$, $qb$Skipping remaining presentation slides to deliver a brief verbal summary and conclude the meeting quickly.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-015$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$In an executive meeting, a leader criticizes your team in a way you consider unfair. You notice your attention shifting from the issue to defending your reputation. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Regain focus on the substance, ask for the specific evidence behind the concern, and respond after separating facts from the personal sting.$qb$, $qb$State that the criticism feels unfair, then explain your team's position.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the meeting ends and address the leader privately.$qb$, $qb$Defend the team's record immediately so silence is not mistaken for agreement.$qb$, $qb$Dismiss the criticism because it was delivered emotionally.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-015$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$In a board meeting, a director questions your integrity after misunderstanding a data point. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Regulate your reaction, correct the misunderstanding calmly, address the integrity implication directly, and offer evidence without personalizing the exchange.$qb$, $qb$Correct the data point and ignore the personal implication.$qb$, $qb$Ask the chair to stop the discussion.$qb$, $qb$Challenge the director's interpretation firmly.$qb$, $qb$End the discussion and send a rebuttal afterward.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-015$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$In a tense investment meeting, criticism of your proposal triggers a strong urge to defend months of work. What action best protects the decision?$qb$, $qb$Notice the reaction, separate personal investment from evidence, and invite the strongest challenge to assumptions before responding.$qb$, $qb$Introduce a sensible safeguard but leave the underlying incentives or decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Run a small corrective action focused mainly on the most visible symptom.$qb$, $qb$Ask individual managers to compensate for the problem through extra effort.$qb$, $qb$Treat the issue as unavoidable and make no material change.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-015$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$At a hostile regulatory hearing, a question wrongly implies your team concealed information. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Manage your reaction, correct the premise precisely, answer the legitimate issue, and avoid turning factual correction into personal confrontation.$qb$, $qb$Take a reasonable immediate action on the visible issue, but leave the wider assumptions and ownership largely unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Gather additional information and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current approach and ask affected teams to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until the problem becomes unambiguous or external pressure forces action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-015$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A board member dismisses a junior executive's valid concern in a way that visibly unsettles the room. You chair the meeting. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Keep the discussion composed, bring the concern back to its evidence and relevance, ensure it is examined fairly, and address meeting behavior without escalating personally.$qb$, $qb$At first, improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$In this case, seek more stakeholder views but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, make a unilateral short-term decision that reduces visible tension but leaves the underlying mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, avoid intervention because changing the current model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R06-015$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A peer publicly questions your competence during a time-critical executive meeting. You have evidence their claim is wrong. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Regulate your response, correct only the material factual point, refocus on the decision, and address the personal conduct separately if needed.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while leaving its core incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder views but do not define how conflicting evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term unilateral decision that reduces visible tension while leaving the underlying mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing the model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R09-015$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$During a board discussion, you realize your irritation with one director is causing you to discount a valid challenge. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Separate the relationship from the argument, restate the challenge fairly, test it against evidence and adjust the decision if warranted.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current model incrementally while retaining its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder input but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the systemic mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing an established model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-015$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A meeting becomes tense and you notice yourself speaking faster and louder. You still need to contribute constructively after the tension. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Slow down, pause, and return to the facts before continuing$qb$, $qb$Take a breath and ask a clarifying question$qb$, $qb$Continue speaking so you can finish your point$qb$, $qb$Leave the meeting without explanation$qb$, $qb$Raise your voice further so others listen$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-015$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A stakeholder challenges your work strongly in a meeting and you feel personally criticized. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Manage your reaction, clarify the concern, and respond to the evidence and impact rather than the tone$qb$, $qb$Pause, ask for a specific example, and answer after understanding it$qb$, $qb$Defend the work immediately so others do not lose confidence$qb$, $qb$Avoid responding until after the meeting$qb$, $qb$Challenge the stakeholder's tone before discussing the issue$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-015$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A difficult stakeholder meeting leaves you frustrated, but you must make an important decision immediately afterward. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Create a brief pause, separate the emotional reaction from the decision facts, and check whether your judgement is being affected$qb$, $qb$Take a short break and review the key facts before deciding$qb$, $qb$Make the decision quickly while the meeting details are fresh$qb$, $qb$Delay every decision until the next day$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to decide because you are frustrated$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R1-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your manager gives you a new urgent task that conflicts with a deadline already agreed. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Explain the conflict and ask which task should take priority$qb$, $qb$Start the urgent task and update the manager about the other deadline$qb$, $qb$Try to complete both without changing either deadline$qb$, $qb$Ask a teammate which task they think is more important$qb$, $qb$Choose the task you personally prefer$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R2-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your manager asks for a quick analysis while you are already preparing material for a meeting in two hours. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Show the timing conflict and confirm which output matters more before changing your plan.$qb$, $qb$Tell the manager when you can realistically start the analysis.$qb$, $qb$Try to squeeze both tasks into the available time without discussing the trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Delay the meeting material and assume the new request is more important.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the new request until the meeting is over.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R3-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your manager asks for a 'short summary,' but the normal template is several pages. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the decision or audience need and provide a concise summary with detail available if required.$qb$, $qb$Send a one-page version and attach the usual template for reference.$qb$, $qb$Complete the normal template because it is the standard format.$qb$, $qb$Remove random sections until the document looks short enough.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the manager to provide exact formatting instructions.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R4-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You finish a task differently from what your manager expected, although the result is correct. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Explain your approach and check the preferred method for future work$qb$, $qb$Show the result and ask whether the approach is acceptable$qb$, $qb$Keep using your method because the result is correct$qb$, $qb$Change the work without discussing why$qb$, $qb$Avoid mentioning the difference$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R5-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your manager's verbal instruction differs from the written task note. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Confirm which instruction should guide the work before proceeding$qb$, $qb$Point out the difference and ask for clarification$qb$, $qb$Follow the verbal instruction because it is newer$qb$, $qb$Follow the written note because it is documented$qb$, $qb$Choose whichever instruction is easier$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R6-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your manager asks for a quick task while you are working on something another manager marked urgent. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Explain the conflict and confirm the priority before changing focus$qb$, $qb$Tell your manager about the other urgent task and ask what they prefer$qb$, $qb$Do the newest request first$qb$, $qb$Continue the current task and ignore the new request$qb$, $qb$Try to do both at the same time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R7-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your manager asks you to 'make the report better' without saying what needs improvement. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask what outcome, audience, or problem the improved report should address$qb$, $qb$Ask which part of the report they want changed$qb$, $qb$Add more detail because that usually improves reports$qb$, $qb$Copy the format of another report$qb$, $qb$Make changes based only on your own preference$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your manager asks for an urgent update while you are waiting for one fact from another team. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Share what is confirmed, clearly state what is pending, and give the expected update time$qb$, $qb$Give the confirmed information and mention the missing fact$qb$, $qb$Wait until the missing fact arrives before replying$qb$, $qb$Guess the missing fact so the update is complete$qb$, $qb$Say the work is delayed without explaining why$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your manager asks for an update and you have good progress but also one new risk. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Share the progress, explain the risk and impact, and state what decision or help is needed$qb$, $qb$Give the progress and flag the new risk clearly$qb$, $qb$Focus on the progress and mention the risk later$qb$, $qb$Report only the risk so it gets attention$qb$, $qb$Say everything is on track$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-REF-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$What is the best course of action when a manager assigns an urgent task that conflicts with a previously set deadline?$qb$, $qb$Confirming priority expectations directly with the manager by presenting workload timelines and trade-offs.$qb$, $qb$Accepting the urgent task immediately and working extended hours to complete both deliverables on schedule.$qb$, $qb$Prioritizing the urgent task right away and notifying the manager about the original missed deadline after it occurs.$qb$, $qb$Asking a colleague to take over the original assignment so you can focus exclusively on the manager's new request.$qb$, $qb$Completing the original task first to honor the initial commitment before beginning the new priority assignment.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your manager wants a single recommendation today; your analysis shows two viable options with different risk profiles and no clearly dominant choice. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Recommend the option that best fits stated priorities, make the trade-offs explicit, and identify what evidence would trigger a change.$qb$, $qb$Present both options neutrally and ask your manager to choose between them.$qb$, $qb$Choose the lower-risk option because uncertainty makes caution preferable.$qb$, $qb$Keep analyzing until one option becomes clearly superior.$qb$, $qb$Select the option you think your manager already prefers.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your board sponsor insists on growth while the CEO has shifted emphasis toward cash generation. Both expect your portfolio plan next week. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Surface the conflict, translate each priority into portfolio consequences, recommend an integrated trade-off, and obtain alignment on decision criteria.$qb$, $qb$Follow the CEO's latest direction because it is operationally authoritative.$qb$, $qb$Keep the growth plan and add a cash-improvement workstream.$qb$, $qb$Prepare two separate plans and let each stakeholder choose.$qb$, $qb$Reduce every initiative's budget proportionally.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$The chief executive asks for a cost reduction that conflicts with a board-approved growth commitment. What is the strongest response?$qb$, $qb$Translate the request into strategic consequences, identify choices that preserve intent, and obtain explicit alignment on remaining trade-offs.$qb$, $qb$Take a practical partial step and escalate the remaining uncertainty to a higher authority.$qb$, $qb$Apply the existing policy more carefully, even though it does not fully address the situation.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that protects the most visible short-term target.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the conflicting evidence and continue with the established position.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$The CEO gives a verbal instruction that appears inconsistent with a recently approved executive-committee decision. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Clarify the intended change, show the conflict and consequences, and confirm the revised priority and governance path before redirecting work.$qb$, $qb$Take a reasonable immediate action on the visible issue, but leave the wider assumptions and ownership largely unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Gather additional information and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current approach and ask affected teams to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until the problem becomes unambiguous or external pressure forces action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your board chair and CEO give different signals about how aggressively to pursue an acquisition. What should you do before mobilizing the deal team?$qb$, $qb$Translate the difference into concrete risk, price and strategic boundaries and obtain explicit alignment through the proper governance route.$qb$, $qb$Improve the existing approach incrementally while leaving its main incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask stakeholders for additional views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term compromise that reduces visible tension without addressing the underlying mechanism.$qb$, $qb$Continue the status quo because changing it could introduce new uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your manager asks for a rapid organizational change that conflicts with commitments you have made to your own leadership team. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify the new priority and rationale, surface the commitments it displaces, recommend a transition, and align before asking your team to change direction.$qb$, $qb$In this case, improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, seek more stakeholder views but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, make a unilateral short-term decision that reduces visible tension but leaves the underlying mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$At first, avoid intervention because changing the current model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your manager asks for a brief summary, but you have prepared a detailed report. The result is correct, but alignment on the method matters for future work. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Send a concise summary with the detailed report available if needed$qb$, $qb$Send the summary and attach the detailed report for reference$qb$, $qb$Send the detailed report because it contains all the facts$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to read the full report first$qb$, $qb$Keep the report until the manager gives clearer instructions$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your manager and a project lead give you conflicting priorities, and both believe their request is urgent. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Make the conflict visible to both, explain the impact, and get an agreed priority before committing$qb$, $qb$Tell your manager about the project lead's request and ask for direction$qb$, $qb$Choose the request with the nearest deadline$qb$, $qb$Try to satisfy both without changing expectations$qb$, $qb$Follow the request from the more senior person without discussion$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-012$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your manager asks you to lead a task but gives you broad freedom, while another senior stakeholder expects a different approach. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Clarify the success criteria and decision boundaries, understand the stakeholder expectation, and align the approach before major work begins$qb$, $qb$Explain the two approaches to your manager and ask which one should guide you$qb$, $qb$Follow your manager's last instruction and ignore the stakeholder$qb$, $qb$Try to satisfy both approaches in parallel$qb$, $qb$Choose the method you personally believe is best$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R1-013$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You join a project and are unsure who owns one part of the work. What should you do first?$qb$, $qb$Clarify the owner before starting that part$qb$, $qb$Ask a teammate how the work is usually divided$qb$, $qb$Start the work and hand it over if needed$qb$, $qb$Wait until someone assigns the task directly$qb$, $qb$Assume the most senior person owns it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R2-013$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$On your first week in a project, two teammates give you different explanations of how a shared task is divided. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Check the agreed project roles with the relevant people and confirm the handoff before starting.$qb$, $qb$Ask the project coordinator which explanation is current.$qb$, $qb$Follow the explanation from the more experienced teammate.$qb$, $qb$Start the work and let someone correct you later.$qb$, $qb$Avoid the shared task until roles become obvious.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R4-013$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two teammates are both working on the same small task without knowing it. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Clarify ownership and divide any remaining work once$qb$, $qb$Tell both people that the work is duplicated$qb$, $qb$Let them finish and use whichever version is better$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to decide whose work should count$qb$, $qb$Say nothing because both versions may be useful$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R5-013$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A project handoff is delayed because everyone thought someone else would send the file. What is the best improvement?$qb$, $qb$Agree one named owner and a clear handoff time for the next cycle$qb$, $qb$Add the handoff to the team checklist$qb$, $qb$Ask everyone to remember the file next time$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to send the file in future$qb$, $qb$Let the receiving team chase the file$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R6-013$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate is absent and their small task blocks the rest of the group. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Check the agreed backup or reassign the task clearly so work can continue$qb$, $qb$Ask who can cover the task and confirm the temporary owner$qb$, $qb$Let several people work on it until someone finishes$qb$, $qb$Wait for the teammate to return$qb$, $qb$Ignore the blocked work$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R7-013$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A shared task is delayed because one teammate did not know their part depended on another person's work. What is the best improvement?$qb$, $qb$Make dependencies and handoff points clear when the work is assigned$qb$, $qb$Add the dependencies to the shared task plan$qb$, $qb$Tell everyone to communicate more often$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to monitor every handoff$qb$, $qb$Let each person manage dependencies themselves$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-013$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A shared task has several small parts, but nobody knows who will combine them into the final version. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Agree one person to assemble the final version and define when each part must reach them$qb$, $qb$Choose a final owner and tell everyone where to send their part$qb$, $qb$Let everyone upload their part and combine them at the end$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to assemble the final version$qb$, $qb$Wait until all parts are finished before deciding$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-013$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate completes their part, but another person does not know the handoff is ready. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Make the handoff visible to the next owner and confirm the next action$qb$, $qb$Notify the next person that the work is ready$qb$, $qb$Assume they will see the status in the system$qb$, $qb$Tell the manager the first part is complete$qb$, $qb$Wait until the next person asks$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-REF-013$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$How should an employee integrate effectively when joining a team with established informal working norms?$qb$, $qb$Observing team communication habits, respecting established norms, and building trust through reliable task delivery.$qb$, $qb$Proposing the adoption of successful communication templates from your previous department during early team meetings.$qb$, $qb$Focusing on delivering assigned tasks independently while participating politely during formal group discussions.$qb$, $qb$Scheduling check-in meetings with senior team members to seek advice on how to stand out within the group.$qb$, $qb$Maintaining your established personal working style, assuming individual deliverable quality is the sole performance driver.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-013$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A matrix team keeps missing handoffs because each function plans capacity independently. More meetings have not helped. What change is most likely to improve performance?$qb$, $qb$Create shared end-to-end planning with visible dependencies, common milestones, and explicit cross-functional ownership for constraints.$qb$, $qb$Increase meeting frequency so emerging conflicts are discussed sooner.$qb$, $qb$Ask each function to commit more contingency capacity to the work.$qb$, $qb$Create a central escalation list for missed handoffs.$qb$, $qb$Hold individuals accountable whenever their local task causes a downstream delay.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-013$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Three acquired businesses must integrate, but each has a strong identity and different operating strengths. What is the best principle?$qb$, $qb$Standardize only enterprise outcomes and interfaces requiring commonality, preserve valuable local strengths, and create mechanisms for interdependent work.$qb$, $qb$Move all three onto the largest unit's operating model.$qb$, $qb$Keep each business independent for a year.$qb$, $qb$Rotate leaders among businesses so integration happens through relationships.$qb$, $qb$Combine teams immediately and let a new culture emerge.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-013$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$After a merger, legacy teams attend common meetings but still route important work through old networks. What should leadership change?$qb$, $qb$Redesign shared workflows, decision rights and measures so important outcomes require cross-legacy collaboration.$qb$, $qb$Act on the strongest available signal while keeping a later review point.$qb$, $qb$Seek broader consensus before making a limited decision.$qb$, $qb$Use a familiar precedent even though important conditions differ.$qb$, $qb$Choose the easiest path to implement regardless of longer-term consequences.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-013$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A newly formed executive team agrees in meetings but repeatedly reopens decisions afterward within functional groups. What should the leader change?$qb$, $qb$Clarify decision rules, record commitments and dissent, and address why leaders feel safer or more rewarded reopening decisions than executing them.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate concern with a sensible local action, then revisit the wider implications at the next formal review.$qb$, $qb$Gather more input and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central ownership or trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current design and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until external evidence makes the problem indisputable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-013$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A cross-company initiative has strong relationships but poor execution because nobody knows who can make final decisions. What intervention matters most?$qb$, $qb$Define outcome ownership, decision rights and escalation boundaries across the participating groups while preserving collaborative input.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, take a useful immediate step on the main issue and schedule the broader structural question for a later governance review.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, gather additional evidence and make a limited adjustment, but leave the central trade-off or ownership unresolved.$qb$, $qb$At first, preserve the current framework and ask the affected leaders to absorb or manage its consequences.$qb$, $qb$In this case, maintain the existing course until the risk becomes certain enough to force a decision.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-014$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your core industry's profit pool may shrink permanently while adjacent services could grow, but those services require capabilities your company lacks. What is the strongest posture?$qb$, $qb$Manage the decline and growth horizons together, invest in capability options and partnerships, define transition signposts and avoid using current profitability to postpone structural repositioning.$qb$, $qb$Apply the closest existing precedent with added safeguards, although it does not fully address the strategic context.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully examining competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest workable compromise without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position even if material risk is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-013$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate is waiting for your input before they can finish their part. Several people's work depends on clear ownership. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Give the needed input by the agreed time or warn them early if it will slip$qb$, $qb$Send what is ready and explain when the rest will follow$qb$, $qb$Finish your other work first because their task is not yours$qb$, $qb$Wait until they remind you$qb$, $qb$Tell them to continue without your input$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-013$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A project is progressing, but two team members are making related decisions independently and their choices may conflict. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Bring the decisions together, clarify ownership and dependencies, and agree how related choices will be coordinated$qb$, $qb$Ask both people to share their decisions before finalizing them$qb$, $qb$Let each person own their area and resolve conflicts later$qb$, $qb$Ask the project manager to approve every related decision$qb$, $qb$Choose one person's approach and ask the other to follow it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-013$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team is meeting individual deadlines, but the final output is repeatedly delayed because integration happens too late. What is the best improvement?$qb$, $qb$Plan integration points during the work, clarify interfaces and owners, and test combined outputs before the final deadline$qb$, $qb$Add earlier handoff dates for each person's work$qb$, $qb$Ask everyone to finish their part sooner$qb$, $qb$Assign one person to combine everything at the end$qb$, $qb$Increase the final deadline buffer$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R2-016$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An AI tool gives a confident answer but provides no source. What should you do before using it?$qb$, $qb$Verify the important facts with a reliable source and take responsibility for the final use.$qb$, $qb$Review the answer carefully and check the most important claim.$qb$, $qb$Use it if it matches what you expected.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI tool to confirm that it is correct.$qb$, $qb$Copy it directly because the wording sounds confident.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R3-016$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An AI tool summarizes a policy correctly except for one date that looks old. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Check the current policy source, correct the date, and review whether the outdated date affects the rest of the summary.$qb$, $qb$Verify the date against the policy and update the summary.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to summarize the policy again.$qb$, $qb$Use the summary with a note that one date may be outdated.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the date because the main explanation seems correct.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R4-016$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An AI summary leaves out a number that appears important in the source document. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Check the source and correct the summary before using it$qb$, $qb$Compare the summary with the relevant source section$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to produce the summary again$qb$, $qb$Use the summary but mention that AI created it$qb$, $qb$Assume the omitted number was not important$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R5-016$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An AI tool gives two different answers to the same factual question. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Check the fact using a reliable independent source before using either answer$qb$, $qb$Compare the two answers and identify where they differ$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI a third time and use the majority answer$qb$, $qb$Choose the answer that sounds more detailed$qb$, $qb$Use the first answer because it came first$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R6-016$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An AI tool suggests a number that does not match the source file. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Use the source file to verify the correct number and investigate the mismatch$qb$, $qb$Check the source and correct the AI output$qb$, $qb$Average the two numbers$qb$, $qb$Use the AI number because it was calculated automatically$qb$, $qb$Ignore the mismatch$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R7-016$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An AI tool creates a list of sources, but one link does not exist. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Verify every important source before relying on the output$qb$, $qb$Check the links that support the main claims$qb$, $qb$Delete the broken link and trust the rest$qb$, $qb$Ask AI to replace the link without checking it$qb$, $qb$Use the list because only one link is wrong$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-016$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An AI-generated answer includes a quotation that you cannot find in the source. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Remove or correct the quotation 
after checking the original source$qb$, $qb$Search the source and verify the quotation before using it$qb$, $qb$Use the quotation without quotation marks$qb$, $qb$Ask AI to confirm the quotation$qb$, $qb$Keep it because the rest of the answer is accurate$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-REF-016$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$What is the correct practice for integrating AI text summarization tools into executive reporting workflows?$qb$, $qb$Auditing AI-generated summaries against raw source data to verify accuracy before finalizing insights.$qb$, $qb$Copying AI-generated summaries directly into final reporting decks to maximize operational processing speed.$qb$, $qb$Analyzing all raw data manually as usual, using AI tools strictly as a secondary check after drafting reports.$qb$, $qb$Forwarding raw dataset files directly into AI tools and distributing generated summaries to leadership.$qb$, $qb$Avoiding AI analytical tools entirely until formal accuracy benchmarks are published by IT departments.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-016$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A business unit wants to automate a high-volume decision using AI. The model performs well on average but poorly on a small, high-impact class of cases. What is the best deployment decision?$qb$, $qb$Design controls for the high-impact cases, set human review thresholds, monitor outcomes by segment, and scale only within those safeguards.$qb$, $qb$Deploy the model broadly because overall accuracy is strong and improve the weak class over time.$qb$, $qb$Use the model only as an advisory tool for all cases and let employees decide when to trust it.$qb$, $qb$Reject the technology until it performs equally well across every case type.$qb$, $qb$Deploy it only for the highest-impact cases because those create the greatest savings.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-016$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your company is considering AI recommendations for workforce reductions using productivity, cost and performance data. What governance approach is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Use AI as decision support with validated data, bias and error testing, suitable explainability, human accountability, appeal routes and monitoring.$qb$, $qb$Let the system rank employees but require executives to approve the final list.$qb$, $qb$Use it only where historical performance data is complete.$qb$, $qb$Delay until AI systems become consistently accurate.$qb$, $qb$Automate decisions because algorithms are less biased than managers.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-016$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An AI forecasting system materially outperforms planners overall but performs poorly during rare market shocks. How should an executive use it?$qb$, $qb$Use its strengths while defining conditions requiring human challenge, alternative scenarios and monitoring for regime changes.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate issue with a reasonable local action, but leave the broader assumptions and ownership largely unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Gather some additional information and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current approach and ask the affected teams to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until the problem becomes unambiguous or externally forces action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-016$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An AI tool flags supplier risk accurately overall, but some high-risk ratings are hard to explain. What should governance require?$qb$, $qb$Match use to consequence: validate data and performance, require reviewable evidence for consequential flags, assign human accountability, and monitor errors.$qb$, $qb$Address the most urgent part of the issue and schedule the broader questions for a later review.$qb$, $qb$Use an existing policy or precedent even though it only partly fits the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the decision upward without first clarifying the trade-offs.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that protects current targets regardless of wider consequences.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-016$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An AI system can identify likely contract risks faster than lawyers but sometimes misses novel clauses. What is the strongest operating model?$qb$, $qb$Use it for triage with validated boundaries, require expert review where novelty or consequence is high, and monitor misses to update controls and models.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate concern with a sensible local action, then revisit the wider implications at the next formal review.$qb$, $qb$Gather more input and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central ownership or trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current design and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until external evidence makes the problem indisputable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-016$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An AI model recommends which customer complaints deserve human investigation. False negatives are rare but could conceal serious safety issues. What design is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Set risk-based human review and override routes, validate rare-event performance, monitor misses, and keep accountable owners for safety-sensitive decisions.$qb$, $qb$In this case, take a useful immediate step on the main issue and schedule the broader structural question for a later governance review.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, gather additional evidence and make a limited adjustment, but leave the central trade-off or ownership unresolved.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, preserve the current framework and ask the affected leaders to absorb or manage its consequences.$qb$, $qb$At first, maintain the existing course until the risk becomes certain enough to force a decision.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R06-016$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An AI system recommends credit limits faster than analysts, but performance varies significantly for new customer segments. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Use segment-specific validation and thresholds, retain accountable human review where evidence is weak or impact high, and monitor outcomes before expanding autonomy.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer the less visible consequences until results can be measured.$qb$, $qb$Use a partial pilot without specifying the evidence or threshold that would determine the next decision.$qb$, $qb$Ask the operational team to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects this year's reported performance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-017$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A twenty-year strategy faces uncertain climate, regulation, demographics and technology simultaneously. What should the planning system do?$qb$, $qb$Focus on robust strategic direction, modular commitments, interacting scenarios, signposts and capital reallocation rules rather than trying to predict a single integrated future.$qb$, $qb$Take a credible immediate action on the visible issue and defer broader institutional implications to the next governance cycle.$qb$, $qb$Gather more evidence and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central enterprise trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences within their areas.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the existing course until external events make change unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-016$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An AI tool produces a spreadsheet formula you do not fully understand. The output may be used in a customer or management decision. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Check how the formula works and test it on known data before using it$qb$, $qb$Test the formula on a few sample values$qb$, $qb$Use it if the result looks reasonable$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI whether the formula is safe$qb$, $qb$Use it in the live file and correct problems later$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-016$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An AI tool produces a useful analysis from company data, but you cannot tell which assumptions drove one conclusion. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify and test the important assumptions before using the conclusion in a decision$qb$, $qb$Review the inputs and ask the tool to explain how it reached the conclusion$qb$, $qb$Use the conclusion if it matches your own expectation$qb$, $qb$Include the conclusion but label it as AI-generated$qb$, $qb$Reject all AI analysis because its reasoning is not fully visible$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-016$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An AI tool recommends a customer action based on historical data, but current market conditions have changed. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Reassess the recommendation using current evidence, test whether the historical pattern still applies, and own the final judgement$qb$, $qb$Add current market information and review how it changes the recommendation$qb$, $qb$Use the AI result because historical data is more objective$qb$, $qb$Reject the AI result because conditions changed$qb$, $qb$Present the AI recommendation without taking a position$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R1-014$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A routine step takes time but no longer seems useful. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask why it is needed and suggest a small test of a simpler method$qb$, $qb$Discuss the concern with the person who owns the process$qb$, $qb$Skip the step once and see whether anyone notices$qb$, $qb$Keep doing it because changing processes is risky$qb$, $qb$Tell coworkers the process is outdated but take no action$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R2-014$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A recurring manual step takes time but does not seem to add value. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask what need the step serves and propose a small safe test of a simpler method.$qb$, $qb$Discuss the step with the process owner and ask whether it can be simplified.$qb$, $qb$Skip the step once and see whether anyone notices.$qb$, $qb$Keep doing it because changing an approved process is risky.$qb$, $qb$Complain that the process is outdated without taking action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R4-014$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A simple recurring task has three manual steps that often cause delays. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Suggest a small safe improvement and test whether it saves time$qb$, $qb$Ask the process owner whether one step can be simplified$qb$, $qb$Skip one step when the workload is high$qb$, $qb$Accept the delay because the process is approved$qb$, $qb$Create a completely new process without checking requirements$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R6-014$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A recurring approval takes days even though most cases are low risk. What is the best improvement approach?$qb$, $qb$Understand why approval is needed and test a simpler path for low-risk cases$qb$, $qb$Discuss whether low-risk cases need the same approval$qb$, $qb$Skip approval for an easy case to prove it is unnecessary$qb$, $qb$Remove the approval step for all cases$qb$, $qb$Accept the delay because approvals reduce risk$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R7-014$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A recurring task requires the same information to be typed into two systems. What is the best first improvement step?$qb$, $qb$Check why both entries are needed and whether one safe shared flow can remove duplication$qb$, $qb$Ask the system owners whether the duplicate entry can be reduced$qb$, $qb$Copy and paste between the systems faster$qb$, $qb$Stop entering the information in one system$qb$, $qb$Accept the duplication because both systems are official$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-014$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A manual check catches almost no issues but takes significant time. What is the best next step?$qb$, $qb$Review the risk the check is meant to control and test a more efficient way to manage it$qb$, $qb$Collect evidence on what the check has caught before suggesting a change$qb$, $qb$Stop the check because it rarely finds problems$qb$, $qb$Automate the check immediately without reviewing its purpose$qb$, $qb$Keep it unchanged because it is part of the process$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-014$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team spends time copying the same status into three places. What is the best first improvement?$qb$, $qb$Find which source is actually needed and test a way to update the information once$qb$, $qb$Map who uses each status copy and why$qb$, $qb$Copy the status faster using a template$qb$, $qb$Stop updating one location without checking$qb$, $qb$Accept the duplication because different teams use it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-REF-014$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$What key action reflects agility when analytics reveal a ongoing operational campaign is underperforming metrics by 30%?$qb$, $qb$Reallocating resources toward high-performing channels based on data analysis and adjusting tactical milestones.$qb$, $qb$Increasing activity frequency on existing channels to offset underperformance through higher volume.$qb$, $qb$Maintaining the current campaign structure until the scheduled end date before conducting a post-mortem review.$qb$, $qb$Escalating underperformance metrics to leadership with a recommendation to halt campaign activities entirely.$qb$, $qb$Adjusting progress report targets downward to align stated benchmarks with actual performance figures.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-014$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A competitor introduces a lower-cost digital service. Your current offer remains profitable, but customer switching has begun in one segment. What is the strongest response?$qb$, $qb$Protect the profitable core while testing differentiated responses in the vulnerable segment and defining evidence-based triggers for broader change.$qb$, $qb$Match the competitor's price across all segments immediately.$qb$, $qb$Wait until switching becomes material before investing in a response.$qb$, $qb$Launch a full replacement product because digital entrants usually scale quickly.$qb$, $qb$Increase marketing spend on the current offer without changing the proposition.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-014$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A technology could reshape your industry's economics, but its adoption curve is highly uncertain. What is the strongest strategic posture?$qb$, $qb$Invest enough to learn and secure options, define signals for scaling or exiting, and avoid commitments dependent on one forecast.$qb$, $qb$Commit early at scale because waiting risks strategic position.$qb$, $qb$Wait until customer demand proves the technology is established.$qb$, $qb$Acquire a specialist company and let it manage the uncertainty.$qb$, $qb$Continue the current strategy until annual planning.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-014$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A mature business faces a smaller entrant whose model makes money differently and serves customers your economics cannot profitably reach. What should you do first?$qb$, $qb$Understand the entrant's economic engine and customer trade-offs, then test responses that need not copy its visible product.$qb$, $qb$Resolve the immediate pressure and document the remaining risk for later review.$qb$, $qb$Use a narrower solution that improves one dimension but leaves key dependencies unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Transfer the decision to another function without clarifying enterprise ownership.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current plan even when evidence shows its assumptions are weakening.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-014$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your successful product may become less relevant if an emerging customer behavior persists, but evidence is mixed. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Protect current value while testing the behavior, investing in options and defining signals that justify larger resource shifts.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core design and incentives intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask the main stakeholders for more input but do not establish how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term decision primarily to restore speed.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the status quo because changing it could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-014$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A disruptive technology could reduce demand for a highly profitable service but also enable a new revenue model your company is poorly equipped to run. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Assess both erosion and creation mechanisms, build capabilities through staged experiments or partnerships, and set triggers for shifting resources between models.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, apply the closest existing precedent with an added safeguard, even though the precedent does not fully address the situation.$qb$, $qb$At first, escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully working through the competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$In this case, choose the quickest workable compromise to restore momentum without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, protect the current target or position even if the cost is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R07-014$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A profitable physical channel is declining slowly while a digital channel grows rapidly but has weaker margins. What is the strongest portfolio response?$qb$, $qb$Analyze customer migration, lifetime economics and capability needs, then stage investment across both channels using signals rather than optimizing only current margin.$qb$, $qb$Use the closest existing precedent with an added safeguard, although it only partly fits the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a senior ruling before fully examining the competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest workable compromise without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target even if risk or cost is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R08-014$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new business model could cannibalize your highest-margin product while attracting customers your current offer cannot serve. What is the strongest strategic response?$qb$, $qb$Model enterprise economics across migration scenarios, test the new proposition and cannibalization assumptions, and allocate investment based on total future value rather than protecting current margin.$qb$, $qb$Apply the nearest existing precedent with an added safeguard, although it only partly fits the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully examining competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the quickest workable compromise without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target even if costs or risks are transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-015$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A board member's challenge is factually weak but reveals a legitimate concern about your own potential conflict of interest. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge and address the conflict transparently, correct factual errors without defensiveness, and ensure the decision process has appropriate independent judgment.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current model incrementally while retaining its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder input but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the systemic mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing an established model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-014$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A small process improvement works well in one task. The improvement may be scaled if the next evidence remains positive. What is the best next step?$qb$, $qb$Check the result, share what worked, and test it in another suitable case$qb$, $qb$Repeat the improvement on a few similar tasks$qb$, $qb$Use it everywhere immediately because the first result was good$qb$, $qb$Keep it private until you have used it for several months$qb$, $qb$Stop using it because one successful test proves little$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-014$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A pilot improvement saves time but creates a new issue for a small group of users. What is the best next step?$qb$, $qb$Keep the useful learning, understand the new issue, and adjust the design before wider rollout$qb$, $qb$Review the affected users' feedback and run another controlled test$qb$, $qb$Roll out the improvement because most users benefit$qb$, $qb$Abandon the improvement because it created a problem$qb$, $qb$Roll it out and let the affected users use the old method$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-014$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A process improvement works for standard cases but performs badly when exceptions occur. What is the best next step?$qb$, $qb$Separate standard and exception paths, learn from the failures, and redesign the improvement before wider adoption$qb$, $qb$Analyze the exception cases and run another limited test$qb$, $qb$Use the improvement only for standard cases permanently$qb$, $qb$Roll it out widely and handle failures when they occur$qb$, $qb$Abandon the improvement because it cannot handle every case$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R1-011$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your team starts using a new process that changes how you do a routine task. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Learn the new steps and use them in your next task$qb$, $qb$Attend the training and ask questions where needed$qb$, $qb$Use both old and new methods until you feel confident$qb$, $qb$Wait to see how experienced teammates handle it$qb$, $qb$Keep using the old method because it is faster$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R3-011$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A familiar task moves to a new system and your first attempt takes longer. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Keep learning the new system, use available guidance, and improve with practice.$qb$, $qb$Review the guidance and try the task again.$qb$, $qb$Return to the old system whenever the new one feels slow.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to use the new system for you.$qb$, $qb$Avoid the task until the new system becomes easier.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R4-011$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A software update changes the screen you use every day. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Learn the new layout and practice the changed steps$qb$, $qb$Read the update notes and try the new screen$qb$, $qb$Keep using an old shortcut until it stops working$qb$, $qb$Ask a teammate to do the changed steps for you$qb$, $qb$Complain about the update and avoid using it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R5-011$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team tool changes and one feature you used often is removed. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Learn the replacement method and adjust your routine$qb$, $qb$Check the guidance and ask about the replacement feature$qb$, $qb$Keep searching for a way to use the old feature$qb$, $qb$Ask a teammate to handle tasks needing that feature$qb$, $qb$Avoid those tasks until the feature returns$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R6-011$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A process changes while you are halfway through a task. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Check which version applies to the current task and adjust accordingly$qb$, $qb$Read the change notice and ask if it applies immediately$qb$, $qb$Finish the task using the old process without checking$qb$, $qb$Restart the whole task using the new process$qb$, $qb$Ignore the change until your next task$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-011$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new workflow changes who approves your work. What should you do before the next submission?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the new approver and follow the updated handoff process$qb$, $qb$Check the updated workflow and ask if anything is unclear$qb$, $qb$Send it to both old and new approvers$qb$, $qb$Keep sending it to the old approver until told directly$qb$, $qb$Choose whichever approver replies faster$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-011$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new work rule is introduced with little notice. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Understand the required change, apply it, and quickly clarify anything that could affect your work$qb$, $qb$Read the rule and ask focused questions where needed$qb$, $qb$Wait for teammates to show how they interpret it$qb$, $qb$Continue the old way until a manager checks$qb$, $qb$Ignore the rule because notice was short$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-REF-011$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Which approach demonstrates high adaptability during a department-wide migration to a new operational platform?$qb$, $qb$Proactively mastering key workflows, creating quick-reference guides for peers, and integrating the platform into daily habits.$qb$, $qb$Attending mandatory training sessions carefully and adjusting daily routines to meet official compliance mandates.$qb$, $qb$Maintaining personal offline tracking spreadsheets while gradually entering required data into the new platform.$qb$, $qb$Suggesting to supervisors that full platform migration be postponed until peak operational cycles pass.$qb$, $qb$Restricting tool usage strictly to mandatory fields while preserving legacy operational habits where possible.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-011$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A regulatory interpretation changes midway through implementation, invalidating part of the design but not the business objective. What is the strongest response?$qb$, $qb$Separate what is now non-compliant from what remains useful, redesign only what is necessary, and reset commitments using the new constraint.$qb$, $qb$Pause the entire program until regulators provide more detailed guidance.$qb$, $qb$Continue the current design while seeking an exemption because significant work is already complete.$qb$, $qb$Implement a temporary manual workaround and leave the underlying design unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Keep the original plan because changing late will create more delivery risk.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-011$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new regulation and a disruptive competitor undermine your business model while a major transformation is already underway. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Reassess the transformation, preserve still-valid investments, stop obsolete work, and redirect capacity through explicit choices.$qb$, $qb$Complete the current transformation before reacting.$qb$, $qb$Launch a second transformation program for the new threats.$qb$, $qb$Ask each function to adapt independently within its budget.$qb$, $qb$Freeze major decisions until both threats are fully understood.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-011$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A sudden trade restriction makes a key component unavailable in one market, but the rule may be temporary. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Protect immediate continuity, identify reversible substitutes and routing options, and set evidence-based triggers for deeper structural changes.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate issue with a reasonable local action, but leave the broader assumptions and ownership largely unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Gather some additional information and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current approach and ask the affected teams to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until the problem becomes unambiguous or externally forces action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-011$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A sanctions change may make an important market inaccessible with little notice. Exiting early destroys value; waiting could strand assets. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Create staged, reversible exposure reductions and explicit legal and commercial triggers while protecting continuity and preparing an orderly exit path.$qb$, $qb$Use the closest existing policy or precedent and add a modest safeguard for this case.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a senior decision without first clarifying the competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest option to restore momentum, even if wider consequences remain unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position and leave secondary impacts to other owners.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-011$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new government suddenly changes industrial policy in a market where you have committed capital but not yet built the facility. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Revalidate the investment against new scenarios, preserve reversible options, engage relevant stakeholders, and stage further commitments against explicit policy signals.$qb$, $qb$At first, apply the closest existing precedent with an added safeguard, even though the precedent does not fully address the situation.$qb$, $qb$In this case, escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully working through the competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, choose the quickest workable compromise to restore momentum without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, protect the current target or position even if the cost is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-012$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$The chair asks you to take an action that the CEO believes exceeds the chair's role, and both expect your loyalty. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Separate personal loyalty from governance, clarify authority and substantive concerns through the proper leadership process, and act only after accountable decision rights are resolved.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer less visible enterprise consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a bounded pilot but leave success criteria and the next decision threshold undefined.$qb$, $qb$Ask relevant operating leaders to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance and reputation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-011$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new process improves control but initially slows your team's work. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Use the new process, identify where the delay occurs, and suggest evidence-based improvements without weakening the control$qb$, $qb$Follow the process and collect examples of where it slows work$qb$, $qb$Create shortcuts for low-risk cases until the process improves$qb$, $qb$Return to the old process when deadlines are tight$qb$, $qb$Complain about the process but make no change$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-011$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new control is introduced after an audit issue, but the first version adds duplicate work. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Follow the control, gather evidence on duplication, and propose a safer streamlined design without weakening the audit requirement$qb$, $qb$Use the control and document where duplicate effort occurs$qb$, $qb$Create an informal shortcut for low-risk cases$qb$, $qb$Continue the old method until the new control is redesigned$qb$, $qb$Ignore the duplication because compliance is more important than efficiency$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-039$qb$, $qb$A1-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You use an AI language model to generate a draft client proposal. How do you ensure the final proposal reflects high quality and strategic precision?$qb$, $qb$Treat AI output as a structural starting draft, rigorously audit data accuracy, inject personal industry insights, and refine tone for stakeholder alignment.$qb$, $qb$Review generated text for basic grammar and formatting, making light edits before submitting the proposal.$qb$, $qb$Use AI only to generate broad outline headings, writing all proposal text manually from scratch.$qb$, $qb$Submit generated proposal text directly to your team lead for review without manual adjustments.$qb$, $qb$Avoid using AI tools for client-facing proposals due to general data privacy concerns.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-085$qb$, $qb$A1-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant proposes three ways to reduce service backlog. One is attractive but ignores a policy constraint that experienced staff know well. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Add the real constraint, reassess the options using both AI analysis and staff context, and take responsibility for the final recommendation.$qb$, $qb$Choose the second-best AI option because the first violates policy.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to produce three new ideas without mentioning the constraint.$qb$, $qb$Reject the AI analysis because it missed an important rule.$qb$, $qb$Use the attractive option for a small trial without telling the policy owner.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1003$qb$, $qb$A1-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI scoring tool favors long candidate responses even when shorter responses contain stronger evidence.$qb$, $qb$Test whether length is acting as an irrelevant proxy, recalibrate around required evidence, and independently review results before trusting the scores.$qb$, $qb$Keep the scores because detail shows effort.$qb$, $qb$Set a minimum response length.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to ignore length and trust the rerun.$qb$, $qb$Discard the tool and score by intuition.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1141$qb$, $qb$A1-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant recommends which customer complaints to escalate based on past escalations. You know the company recently changed its escalation policy. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Evaluate recommendations against the current policy, identify where historical patterns no longer apply, and update or constrain AI use before relying on it.$qb$, $qb$Use the AI because past escalation decisions are useful precedent.$qb$, $qb$Ignore all AI recommendations until enough new history exists.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to follow the new policy and trust the next output.$qb$, $qb$Escalate every complaint to avoid missing one.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1187$qb$, $qb$A1-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant drafts a response to a customer complaint and confidently states that the company caused the problem, but the investigation is not complete. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Remove the unsupported attribution, state only verified facts and appropriate empathy, and keep responsibility wording aligned with the completed investigation.$qb$, $qb$Send it because accepting responsibility may calm the customer.$qb$, $qb$Change 'caused' to 'may have caused' and send without checking other claims.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to make the response more neutral and trust the new draft.$qb$, $qb$Do not respond to the customer until the investigation is complete.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1279$qb$, $qb$A1-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant suggests rejecting a customer application because similar past applications had high failure rates, but the current application includes new evidence not present historically. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Evaluate the new evidence directly against current criteria, understand what the AI comparison omits, and use historical similarity only as one input rather than the decision itself.$qb$, $qb$Reject it because historical failure is strong evidence.$qb$, $qb$Approve it because the application contains new evidence.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to rerun the score and trust any changed result.$qb$, $qb$Ignore all historical patterns.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-131$qb$, $qb$A1-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$AI recommends prioritizing a group of customers based on past behavior. A new policy has changed what offers can be made to some of those customers. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Apply the current policy constraints, reassess the ranking and expected outcomes, and use AI insight only within the real operating context.$qb$, $qb$Use the AI ranking but manually remove customers you know are affected.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the AI ranking because policy changed.$qb$, $qb$Ask AI to interpret the policy and follow its revised ranking without review.$qb$, $qb$Use the original ranking because it is based on historical data.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1325$qb$, $qb$A1-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant recommends declining a request because the applicant's profile differs from typical approved cases, but the policy allows several valid pathways. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess the request against the actual policy pathways and evidence, understand why the AI treated difference as risk, and do not equate atypicality with ineligibility.$qb$, $qb$Decline because typical approved profiles are safer benchmarks.$qb$, $qb$Approve because unusual profiles deserve flexibility.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to compare only with the most similar approved case.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the policy and rely on your intuition.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1417$qb$, $qb$A1-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant recommends a standard response to a customer based on similar past cases, but the customer has a contractual term that differs from the standard. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check the customer's actual contract, adapt the response to the applicable term, and treat the AI precedent as supporting context rather than authority.$qb$, $qb$Use the standard response because similar cases should be handled consistently.$qb$, $qb$Reject the AI response and write from memory.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to read the contract and trust its revised answer.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the contractual difference if it is minor.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1509$qb$, $qb$A1-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant suggests a credit limit based on past customers of similar size, but the current customer has unusually strong verified payment history. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess the verified payment evidence and current credit criteria directly, using the AI comparison as context rather than allowing similarity to override relevant new information.$qb$, $qb$Use the AI limit because customer size is the strongest benchmark.$qb$, $qb$Raise the limit automatically because payment history is strong.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to rerun with payment history and trust the result.$qb$, $qb$Ignore all historical comparisons.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1555$qb$, $qb$A1-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant recommends denying a warranty claim because similar past claims were outside coverage, but this customer purchased an optional extension. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check the customer's actual warranty terms and evidence, apply the extension correctly, and use the AI comparison only as background rather than the decision rule.$qb$, $qb$Deny it because similar claims were not covered.$qb$, $qb$Approve it automatically because an extension exists.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to include the extension and trust its new answer.$qb$, $qb$Ignore all historical claim patterns.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1601$qb$, $qb$A1-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant flags a transaction as unusual because it is much larger than past transactions, but the approved contract includes a planned annual bulk purchase. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check the transaction against the contract and current approval evidence, treat the AI anomaly as a prompt for review, and clear or escalate it based on the actual context.$qb$, $qb$Block it because unusual amounts are risky.$qb$, $qb$Approve it automatically because the contract mentions a bulk purchase.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to lower the anomaly score.$qb$, $qb$Ignore anomaly detection for this customer in future.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-175$qb$, $qb$A1-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant recommends one course of action while an experienced colleague recommends another. Both sound reasonable, and you do not yet have enough evidence to know who is right. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare the assumptions and evidence behind both views, test the disputed points against reliable information, and make the final decision with clear human accountability.$qb$, $qb$Ask both the colleague and the AI to explain their reasoning in more detail, then choose the argument that is better supported.$qb$, $qb$Prefer the colleague's view because practical experience is more reliable than generated advice.$qb$, $qb$Prefer the AI recommendation because it can consider more information than one person.$qb$, $qb$Split the difference between the two recommendations so neither perspective is ignored.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2153$qb$, $qb$A1-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$AI classifies a customer from historical behavior, but the customer's business model changed recently. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check current evidence and criteria, account for the business-model change, and use AI history only as context.$qb$, $qb$Apply a manual override for this specific case while leaving AI decision rules unadjusted.$qb$, $qb$Accept the AI output without review because automated models process larger historical datasets.$qb$, $qb$Disable the AI tool entirely and revert to fully manual processing for all cases.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the contextual discrepancy and proceed with the automated AI recommendation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-221$qb$, $qb$A1-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant drafts a strong analysis but cites two sources you cannot verify. The conclusion is plausible and your deadline is close. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Verify the cited claims independently, remove or replace unsupported evidence, and use the AI draft only after the analysis can be defended from reliable sources.$qb$, $qb$Keep the analysis but remove the two citations you cannot verify.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to provide replacement sources and use them if they look credible.$qb$, $qb$Use the draft with a note that some sources were generated by AI.$qb$, $qb$Submit it because the conclusion is plausible and the deadline is close.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2567$qb$, $qb$A1-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant recommends reducing stock because historical demand is falling, but a new promotion starts next week. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use the promotion forecast and current demand evidence, treating the AI's historical trend as one input rather than the final stock decision.$qb$, $qb$Apply a manual override for this specific case while leaving AI decision rules unadjusted.$qb$, $qb$Accept the AI output without review because automated models process larger historical datasets.$qb$, $qb$Disable the AI tool entirely and revert to fully manual processing for all cases.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the contextual discrepancy and proceed with the automated AI recommendation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-267$qb$, $qb$A1-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant drafts interview notes from a transcript but attributes one important statement to the wrong speaker. The rest appears accurate. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Verify decision-relevant notes against the transcript, correct the attribution, check for similar errors, and treat the AI draft as an aid rather than the authoritative record.$qb$, $qb$Correct the one attribution you noticed and use the remaining notes.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to regenerate the notes and use the second version if the attribution is fixed.$qb$, $qb$Use the notes but mention that AI produced them.$qb$, $qb$Discard all AI assistance for interview notes because one attribution error makes the tool unreliable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2751$qb$, $qb$A1-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$AI forecasts low demand from recent history, but a confirmed customer launch adds volume next month. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use the confirmed launch and current assumptions, treating the AI historical forecast only as a baseline.$qb$, $qb$Apply a manual override for this specific case while leaving AI decision rules unadjusted.$qb$, $qb$Accept the AI output without review because automated models process larger historical datasets.$qb$, $qb$Disable the AI tool entirely and revert to fully manual processing for all cases.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the contextual discrepancy and proceed with the automated AI recommendation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2833$qb$, $qb$A1-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant recommends a candidate because their resume closely matches previous successful hires, but the role now requires a new skill those hires did not need. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Evaluate the candidate against the current role requirements, treat historical similarity only as supporting input, and ensure the new required skill is assessed directly.$qb$, $qb$Follow the AI because previous successful hires are the best benchmark.$qb$, $qb$Reject the candidate because the AI model is outdated.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to increase the weight of the new skill and trust the rerun.$qb$, $qb$Ignore all historical hiring evidence.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-313$qb$, $qb$A1-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant suggests a polished response to a dissatisfied customer, but it promises an action your team has not confirmed it can deliver. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Remove the unsupported promise, verify what the team can commit to, and rewrite the response so the AI's useful wording is retained only where it matches real authority and context.$qb$, $qb$Send the response after changing the promise to less definite wording.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to make the promise more cautious and use its next version.$qb$, $qb$Send it because a confident response may reassure the customer while the team works out delivery.$qb$, $qb$Discard the entire AI draft and write every customer response manually.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-359$qb$, $qb$A1-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant suggests three possible causes for a technical problem. One is unfamiliar to you but plausible. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use the suggestions as hypotheses, test them against system evidence and safe diagnostics, and investigate the unfamiliar cause without assuming the AI has identified the answer.$qb$, $qb$Start with the unfamiliar cause because AI may notice patterns humans miss.$qb$, $qb$Test only the two causes you already understand.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI which cause is most likely and investigate that one first.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the AI suggestions and troubleshoot only from your own experience.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-405$qb$, $qb$A1-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant produces a good first draft of a policy explanation, but employees may interpret one sentence as a promise the policy does not actually make. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the ambiguous sentence against the authoritative policy, review other decision-sensitive wording, and use the AI draft only after human contextual checking.$qb$, $qb$Change the one sentence and send the rest of the draft unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to make the sentence less definite and use its revision.$qb$, $qb$Add a disclaimer that the explanation is only a summary.$qb$, $qb$Discard the draft and stop using AI for policy communication.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-451$qb$, $qb$A1-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant gives you a concise summary of a long email thread, but it omits a disagreement that could affect the final decision. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Restore the material disagreement from the source thread, verify other decision-critical points, and use the AI summary only after it accurately represents both agreement and unresolved issues.$qb$, $qb$Add the disagreement you noticed and use the rest of the summary.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to create a more detailed summary and use that instead.$qb$, $qb$Attach the original thread and keep the concise AI summary unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Stop using AI for email summaries because omissions are unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-497$qb$, $qb$A1-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant suggests wording for a sensitive employee message that is factually correct but sounds unusually cold. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Keep any useful factual structure, rewrite the tone using human context and empathy, and verify that the final message remains accurate and appropriate for the recipient.$qb$, $qb$Send the message because factual clarity matters more than tone.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to make it warmer and send the next version without further review.$qb$, $qb$Add a friendly greeting and keep the rest unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Stop using AI for sensitive communication entirely.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-543$qb$, $qb$A1-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant proposes a meeting agenda that covers every topic but allocates most time to low-priority updates and little time to a difficult decision. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Reorder and resize the agenda around the meeting's actual decision needs, keep useful AI-generated topics where relevant, and apply human judgment to time allocation.$qb$, $qb$Use the agenda but ask participants to move faster through the update sections.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to create another agenda and choose the better version.$qb$, $qb$Use the agenda because it comprehensively covers all topics.$qb$, $qb$Discard AI-generated agendas and create every agenda manually.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-635$qb$, $qb$A1-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant drafts a project risk list that includes several generic risks but misses a dependency you know is critical in this project. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use the draft as a starting checklist, add the project-specific dependency, challenge generic items against the actual context, and complete the risk review with human knowledge.$qb$, $qb$Add the missing dependency and keep all AI-generated risks.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to generate a longer list until the dependency appears.$qb$, $qb$Discard the AI list because it missed an important risk.$qb$, $qb$Use the AI list because standardized risks provide consistency across projects.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-681$qb$, $qb$A1-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant produces a polished executive summary but gives equal space to minor details and the one issue that could change the decision. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Restructure the summary around decision significance, elevate the material issue, keep useful supporting detail selectively, and verify the final emphasis against the source evidence.$qb$, $qb$Use the AI summary and bold the material issue.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to shorten the summary and assume important points will remain.$qb$, $qb$Keep equal coverage because balanced summaries should not emphasize one issue.$qb$, $qb$Discard the summary and avoid AI for executive communication.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-727$qb$, $qb$A1-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant suggests a concise answer to a technical question, but you know there are two valid approaches depending on the user's environment. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use the draft as a base, add the environmental distinction, explain when each approach applies, and verify the technical details before sending.$qb$, $qb$Send the AI answer because concise guidance is easier for users.$qb$, $qb$Choose the approach you personally use and remove the other possibility.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to decide which approach is more common.$qb$, $qb$Discard the AI response because it failed to mention both approaches.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-773$qb$, $qb$A1-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant proposes a performance summary that labels an employee's result 'poor,' but the underlying data shows mixed outcomes and important context. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Replace the broad label with evidence-based observations, include material context, and use the AI draft only where its wording accurately reflects the underlying record.$qb$, $qb$Change 'poor' to 'below expectations' and keep the rest.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to generate a more balanced summary.$qb$, $qb$Use the summary because performance labels help managers make decisions quickly.$qb$, $qb$Discard all AI assistance for performance summaries.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-819$qb$, $qb$A1-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant suggests five interview questions, but two appear to test the same behavior in slightly different words. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the intended evidence for each question, remove or redesign redundant items, and use AI suggestions only where they add distinct, role-relevant evidence.$qb$, $qb$Use all five because repeated behaviors improve reliability.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to generate five more and choose the most varied wording.$qb$, $qb$Keep one of the two duplicate questions at random.$qb$, $qb$Discard the entire AI-generated set.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-084$qb$, $qb$A1-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A pilot project has a clear business objective, but detailed requirements will be decided after users test early versions. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Define near-term outcomes and assumptions, build reversible steps, create frequent feedback points, and update the plan as evidence develops.$qb$, $qb$Write a complete requirement set based on your best assumptions and proceed.$qb$, $qb$Wait until users provide final requirements before doing meaningful work.$qb$, $qb$Ask senior leaders to make all open decisions.$qb$, $qb$Focus only on tasks that are completely certain and leave the rest unplanned.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1278$qb$, $qb$A1-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A new workflow performs well during normal demand, but it has not been tested during the annual peak that doubles volume. You must advise whether to rely on it for peak season. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use current performance as positive evidence, assess capacity and failure points at peak volume, and run a load test or controlled contingency plan before relying on it fully.$qb$, $qb$Rely on it because normal performance is strong.$qb$, $qb$Reject it because peak performance is unknown.$qb$, $qb$Assume performance will be half as fast at double volume.$qb$, $qb$Ask the team whether they feel confident about peak season.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1324$qb$, $qb$A1-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A new process performs well in two small offices, but the next rollout site handles ten times the transaction volume. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Treat the small-site evidence as encouraging, assess capacity and bottlenecks at the larger scale, and use load testing, staged rollout or contingency before full reliance.$qb$, $qb$Roll out fully because two sites succeeded.$qb$, $qb$Reject rollout because the large site is different.$qb$, $qb$Assume performance will scale linearly.$qb$, $qb$Ask the large site's manager whether they are comfortable trying it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-174$qb$, $qb$A1-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are asked to explore a new opportunity, but the objective is broad, there is no established process, and several important facts are still unknown. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Define what is known and unknown, agree a near-term success criterion, take small learning steps, and update stakeholders as the direction becomes clearer.$qb$, $qb$Choose a reasonable working direction, document assumptions, and schedule regular check-ins.$qb$, $qb$Collect as much information as possible before taking action so the uncertainty is reduced first.$qb$, $qb$Ask for a detailed brief before beginning because broad objectives create too much rework risk.$qb$, $qb$Wait for someone else to establish a process because loosely defined work often changes later.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-220$qb$, $qb$A1-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are asked to prepare an initial plan for a project whose final budget and scope will not be confirmed for another month. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Build a provisional plan around confirmed facts, label assumptions and decision points, create options for different budget or scope outcomes, and update the plan as uncertainty resolves.$qb$, $qb$Prepare one likely plan and revise it when budget and scope are confirmed.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the final budget before doing detailed planning.$qb$, $qb$Ask the sponsor to provide a fixed scope before you begin.$qb$, $qb$Create a detailed plan using your best assumptions and present it as the baseline.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2428$qb$, $qb$A1-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A pilot scheduling process works well when all staff are in one location. The next site has employees across several time zones. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Treat the pilot as useful evidence but test time-zone constraints, handoffs and exception handling before full rollout.$qb$, $qb$Respond to scheduling process works well when all with the standard explanation before clarifying the other person's actual need.$qb$, $qb$Add more detail about scheduling process works well when all even though the problem is relevance or clarity rather than missing information.$qb$, $qb$Ask another colleague to take over scheduling process works well when all without preserving the context already available.$qb$, $qb$Treat the concern about scheduling process works well when all as resistance and continue with the original message.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-266$qb$, $qb$A1-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are asked to estimate effort for a possible project before the final requirements are known. The estimate will be used only to decide whether further discovery is worthwhile. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Create a range based on known scope, state the major assumptions and uncertainty drivers, and identify what discovery would most improve the estimate.$qb$, $qb$Give a single best estimate based on similar projects and revise it later.$qb$, $qb$Refuse to estimate until final requirements are available.$qb$, $qb$Use the highest plausible estimate so the organization is protected from under-budgeting.$qb$, $qb$Ask a more experienced colleague to provide the estimate instead.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2832$qb$, $qb$A1-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A trial shows strong productivity improvement, but it ran during an unusually quiet business period. You must advise whether to adopt it permanently. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$State the positive result and the workload limitation, identify what could change under normal volume, and recommend a targeted higher-volume test or bounded rollout.$qb$, $qb$Adopt it because productivity improved strongly.$qb$, $qb$Reject it because the trial conditions were unusual.$qb$, $qb$Assume the effect will be smaller but still positive.$qb$, $qb$Ask the sponsor to decide whether the trial is good enough.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-312$qb$, $qb$A1-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are asked to investigate why a customer segment is declining, but there is no agreed hypothesis and only partial data is available. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Frame the main plausible questions, identify the most decision-relevant data gaps, analyze what is available, and update the investigation as evidence strengthens or weakens each explanation.$qb$, $qb$Choose the most likely explanation from experience and test it first.$qb$, $qb$Collect all missing data before beginning any analysis.$qb$, $qb$Ask the sponsor to define the likely cause before you start.$qb$, $qb$Produce a broad report covering every possible cause equally.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-358$qb$, $qb$A1-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are asked for an initial recommendation on a new partnership, but commercial terms and final responsibilities are still being negotiated. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$State the recommendation conditionally, identify the assumptions it depends on, define the terms or responsibilities that would change it, and update the view as negotiations progress.$qb$, $qb$Give your best recommendation now and revise it if the final agreement changes.$qb$, $qb$Wait until all terms are final before offering any recommendation.$qb$, $qb$Recommend against the partnership because unresolved responsibilities create too much uncertainty.$qb$, $qb$Ask the negotiation team to make the recommendation because they know the terms best.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-404$qb$, $qb$A1-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are asked whether a small pilot should continue, but results are mixed and the sample is too small for a firm conclusion. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Explain what the pilot does and does not show, identify the most important uncertainty, and recommend a bounded next test or stop decision based on the cost of learning more.$qb$, $qb$Recommend continuing because some results are positive.$qb$, $qb$Recommend stopping because the pilot failed to prove success.$qb$, $qb$Wait until a much larger sample is available before offering any view.$qb$, $qb$Ask the sponsor to decide because the evidence is inconclusive.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-542$qb$, $qb$A1-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer asks whether a feature idea is feasible. Engineering has not estimated it, but you understand the general architecture and major unknowns. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Give a bounded preliminary view, state the technical assumptions and unknowns, and explain what engineering check is needed before any firm commitment.$qb$, $qb$Say it is probably feasible based on your understanding of the architecture.$qb$, $qb$Refuse to discuss feasibility until engineering completes an estimate.$qb$, $qb$Promise to explore it because most feature requests can eventually be built.$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer to submit a formal request and give no preliminary view.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-588$qb$, $qb$A1-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You need to recommend whether to continue an experiment. Early results are promising, but an important customer segment has not yet participated. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$State what the current results support, identify the missing segment as a material uncertainty, and recommend a bounded continuation or targeted test based on the cost and value of learning more.$qb$, $qb$Recommend continuing because the available results are promising.$qb$, $qb$Stop the experiment until the missing segment participates.$qb$, $qb$Treat the current results as representative unless later evidence contradicts them.$qb$, $qb$Ask the sponsor to decide because the evidence is incomplete.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-634$qb$, $qb$A1-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are asked to give an early view on whether a new vendor is suitable. References are positive, but security review and final pricing are incomplete. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Give a conditional view based on what is known, identify security and price as decision gates, and explain what outcomes would change the recommendation.$qb$, $qb$Recommend the vendor because the references are positive.$qb$, $qb$Reject the vendor until every review is complete.$qb$, $qb$Wait to provide any view until final pricing arrives.$qb$, $qb$Ask procurement to decide because too many facts are still missing.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-864$qb$, $qb$A1-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A new customer segment shows strong interest but very little purchase history. You are asked whether to increase marketing spend. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Give a provisional view based on interest quality, acquisition cost and available conversion evidence, and recommend a bounded spend test that resolves the most important commercial uncertainty.$qb$, $qb$Increase spend because interest is strong.$qb$, $qb$Keep spend unchanged until enough purchase history exists.$qb$, $qb$Stop targeting the segment because conversion is unproven.$qb$, $qb$Ask marketing leadership to decide based on experience.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-037$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A legacy project management methodology you have relied on for years is officially replaced by an agile framework across your department. How do you adapt?$qb$, $qb$Consciously set aside legacy habits, immerse yourself in new agile practices, seek feedback on implementation, and champion adaptation.$qb$, $qb$Adopt new agile terms during team meetings while maintaining your familiar tracking methods in personal notes.$qb$, $qb$Request permission from your manager to continue using legacy methods for ongoing projects while applying agile to new ones.$qb$, $qb$Attend agile workshops and wait for senior team members to demonstrate how agile applies to daily tasks.$qb$, $qb$Compare agile drawbacks against legacy benefits during team discussions to highlight migration risks.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-083$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your team adopts a new shared workflow that removes several personal tracking steps you have used successfully for years. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Understand what the shared workflow replaces, verify that important controls are covered, and deliberately stop duplicating old habits once the new method proves reliable.$qb$, $qb$Use both methods permanently for important work.$qb$, $qb$Follow the new workflow but keep your private tracker as the real source of truth.$qb$, $qb$Ask for permission to continue the old method because it works for you.$qb$, $qb$Wait to see whether other experienced colleagues fully adopt the new workflow.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1139$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$In your previous team, every small purchase required manager approval. In your current team, policy gives you a defined spending limit. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use the delegated limit for appropriate purchases, document them as required, and seek approval only when the amount or risk falls outside your authority.$qb$, $qb$Keep asking the manager for every purchase until they tell you to stop.$qb$, $qb$Use the limit without keeping any records.$qb$, $qb$Spend slightly above the limit if the purchase is urgent.$qb$, $qb$Ask colleagues whether they think approval is needed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1185$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are used to attending every meeting involving your project. In a new role, many meetings are informational and recordings are available. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Attend where your decision, relationship or live input is needed, use recordings or notes for routine information, and stop treating attendance itself as involvement.$qb$, $qb$Keep attending everything until you know the role better.$qb$, $qb$Stop attending all informational meetings.$qb$, $qb$Join meetings but work on other tasks at the same time.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager to decide every meeting you should attend.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1231$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You previously required a detailed weekly report from every team member. Your new team has reliable live work tracking and only a few exceptions need discussion.$qb$, $qb$Stop duplicating routine status, define the exceptions or context you still need, and use team time for decisions and support rather than recreating the old report.$qb$, $qb$Keep the report until the team asks to stop.$qb$, $qb$Require a shorter report from everyone.$qb$, $qb$Stop all status communication.$qb$, $qb$Ask each employee to choose their own reporting method.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1277$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$In your previous role, you reviewed every team member's work before it went to a customer. Your current team has experienced staff with defined quality authority. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use the current authority model, review exceptions and higher-risk work, and stop inserting yourself into every routine output simply because that was your old habit.$qb$, $qb$Keep reviewing everything until you personally trust each person.$qb$, $qb$Stop reviewing any work at all.$qb$, $qb$Ask staff to copy you on every customer message instead.$qb$, $qb$Let only the most experienced employee send work without your review.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-129$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A new shared system makes a personal spreadsheet you built over years unnecessary. You trust your spreadsheet more because you understand it completely. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Verify that the shared system covers the required needs, compare results during a short transition, then retire the duplicate tool once confidence is established.$qb$, $qb$Keep both systems permanently for safety.$qb$, $qb$Use the shared system only for reporting and keep your spreadsheet for real work.$qb$, $qb$Ask to be exempted from the shared system.$qb$, $qb$Wait until a colleague proves the shared system is better.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1415$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are used to personally checking every agenda before a team member chairs a meeting. In your new team, experienced employees are expected to manage routine meetings independently. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Set clear expectations for outcomes and escalate only higher-risk meetings, allowing experienced employees to prepare routine agendas without your pre-approval.$qb$, $qb$Keep checking agendas until each person proves themselves repeatedly.$qb$, $qb$Stop giving any guidance on meetings.$qb$, $qb$Ask employees to copy you on every agenda but do not review it.$qb$, $qb$Chair important parts yourself.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1507$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are used to preparing detailed slides before every decision discussion. Your current team often makes routine decisions from a short written note. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use the written note where it provides the required evidence and trade-offs, and reserve slides for decisions that genuinely benefit from visual or live explanation.$qb$, $qb$Keep preparing slides because important decisions deserve presentations.$qb$, $qb$Stop using slides for any decision.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager to choose the format each time.$qb$, $qb$Prepare both notes and slides for every decision.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1553$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are used to asking for a formal presentation before approving small process changes. Your current team uses short test results and decision notes for low-risk changes. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use the lighter evidence where it supports the decision, and reserve formal presentations for changes whose complexity or stakeholder needs justify them.$qb$, $qb$Keep presentations because formal review improves discipline.$qb$, $qb$Stop using presentations for all changes.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager which format to use every time.$qb$, $qb$Require both a decision note and presentation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1599$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are used to asking for a full written business case before trying any process improvement. Your current team can run small reversible tests at very low cost. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use proportionate analysis and a bounded test for low-risk reversible ideas, reserving full business cases for decisions whose cost or irreversibility justifies them.$qb$, $qb$Keep requiring full business cases because discipline prevents mistakes.$qb$, $qb$Stop documenting improvement decisions.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager to approve every test.$qb$, $qb$Run tests without defining success measures.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-173$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A method that made you successful in a previous role is producing poor results in your current team, even after you have used it several times. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify which assumptions behind the old method no longer fit, stop or modify the habit, and deliberately practice an approach better suited to the current context.$qb$, $qb$Adapt the method while keeping the parts that still appear useful and monitor whether results improve.$qb$, $qb$Give the method more time because proven approaches often need consistent use before others adjust.$qb$, $qb$Ask former colleagues how they would make the old method work in this environment.$qb$, $qb$Conclude that the current team is not ready for the higher standard the method requires.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-219$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are used to solving urgent issues personally, but in your new role this habit is preventing your team from learning to handle them. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize that the old habit no longer fits your role, define which incidents others should own, coach them through early cases, and resist taking work back when their approach differs from yours.$qb$, $qb$Delegate more urgent issues but remain closely involved in every decision.$qb$, $qb$Continue solving the most serious issues yourself and delegate only routine ones.$qb$, $qb$Tell the team they must handle urgent issues from now on without your help.$qb$, $qb$Keep the current approach because speed is more important than development during urgent work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-265$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$In your previous team, copying the manager on most decisions helped work move quickly. In your new team, colleagues say this makes them feel bypassed. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize that the old escalation habit does not fit the new team's decision norms, clarify when manager involvement is actually needed, and work directly with peers for routine decisions.$qb$, $qb$Copy the manager less often but continue doing it on decisions you consider important.$qb$, $qb$Explain that copying the manager is only for visibility and keep the practice.$qb$, $qb$Stop copying the manager on everything, including genuine escalations.$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to tell the team that your approach is acceptable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-311$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You have always prepared detailed meeting packs because a former manager expected them. Your current stakeholders rarely read them and ask for shorter decision notes. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Stop treating the old expectation as the default, learn what the current stakeholders need, and redesign the pack around concise decision information while preserving genuinely necessary detail.$qb$, $qb$Shorten the pack slightly but keep the same overall structure.$qb$, $qb$Continue the detailed pack and add a one-page summary.$qb$, $qb$Ask the former manager's team whether detailed packs are still considered best practice.$qb$, $qb$Keep producing the full pack because thorough preparation is always safer.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-403$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are used to measuring productivity by hours spent, but your new team works flexibly and is judged mainly on completed outcomes. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Shift your own planning and feedback toward agreed outputs, quality and deadlines, and stop using visible hours as the default signal of commitment.$qb$, $qb$Continue tracking hours privately while reporting outcomes formally.$qb$, $qb$Use both hours and outcomes equally until you decide which is more reliable.$qb$, $qb$Ask the team to keep standard hours so productivity is easier to compare.$qb$, $qb$Assume flexible working makes productivity impossible to judge accurately.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-449$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You previously succeeded by personally checking every important deliverable. Your current role now covers too many projects for that approach to scale. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Replace personal checking with clear quality criteria, capable ownership and risk-based review points, and learn to rely on the system rather than your direct inspection of everything.$qb$, $qb$Check only the highest-value deliverables personally and keep the old approach there.$qb$, $qb$Work longer so you can continue checking everything important.$qb$, $qb$Stop checking all deliverables and leave quality entirely to project owners.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager which deliverables you should continue checking personally.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-541$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You have always required people to copy you on important emails so you stay informed. In your new role, the volume is making you miss genuinely important messages. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Stop using blanket copying as the default, define which decisions or risks genuinely require your visibility, and rely on shared records or summaries for routine information.$qb$, $qb$Ask people to mark important copied emails as urgent.$qb$, $qb$Keep being copied but create more email filters.$qb$, $qb$Tell people to stop copying you on everything and contact you only when something goes wrong.$qb$, $qb$Continue the practice because missing context would be worse than email overload.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-771$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You have always asked team members for detailed written updates before one-to-one meetings. In your new team, live work systems already show most status information. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Stop duplicating status data, ask only for context or reflection the systems cannot show, and use one-to-one time for decisions, support and development.$qb$, $qb$Keep a shorter written update so people prepare properly.$qb$, $qb$Continue the detailed updates until you know the team better.$qb$, $qb$Stop all preparation and discuss whatever comes up in the meeting.$qb$, $qb$Ask each employee whether they personally prefer written updates.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-863$qb$, $qb$A1-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You have always asked staff to submit weekly timesheets for project visibility. The new project system already records work progress and time automatically. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm whether the timesheet serves any remaining payroll, compliance or planning purpose, and remove duplicate manual entry if those needs are already met.$qb$, $qb$Keep timesheets because manual confirmation improves accuracy.$qb$, $qb$Stop timesheets immediately because automation exists.$qb$, $qb$Reduce timesheets to monthly without checking their purpose.$qb$, $qb$Ask employees whether they prefer manual or automatic tracking.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-015$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$During a live client negotiation, a technical system glitch disrupts your presentation, causing momentary confusion among stakeholders. How do you maintain composure?$qb$, $qb$Pause calmly, acknowledge the glitch lightheartedly, transition seamlessly to an offline summary slide, and guide the presentation forward.$qb$, $qb$Apologize repeatedly for the technical difficulty while quickly working to fix the presentation file on screen.$qb$, $qb$Ask a team colleague to take over speaking duties while you troubleshoot the presentation software in the background.$qb$, $qb$Suggest pausing the meeting for 10 minutes so everyone can reconvene after technical issues are resolved.$qb$, $qb$Skip the slide deck entirely and deliver a rapid verbal summary to complete the meeting as fast as possible.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-061$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$In a tense meeting, a stakeholder says your team 'never thinks ahead.' You feel defensive because you believe the statement is unfair. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Notice your reaction, ask for the specific concern behind the statement, and respond to the evidence and next action rather than the personal tone.$qb$, $qb$Tell the stakeholder you disagree but are willing to hear examples.$qb$, $qb$Explain the planning work your team already does so others do not accept the criticism.$qb$, $qb$End the discussion and suggest continuing privately later.$qb$, $qb$Stay silent for the rest of the meeting to avoid saying something unhelpful.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-107$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$After receiving unexpected criticism, you notice yourself repeatedly thinking about it while preparing for an important customer call. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Create a short reset, identify any useful point to revisit later, deliberately refocus on the customer objective, and avoid carrying the earlier emotion into the call.$qb$, $qb$Tell the customer you had a difficult meeting and may be distracted.$qb$, $qb$Keep working and hope concentration returns.$qb$, $qb$Send a defensive response to the critic before the customer call.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to take the customer call.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-197$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$During a difficult discussion, you notice that you are preparing your rebuttal instead of listening to the person speaking. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Notice the shift, stop composing your response mentally, return attention to the speaker's current point, and check your understanding before replying.$qb$, $qb$Let the person finish and then use the rebuttal you have prepared.$qb$, $qb$Write down your counterpoints while they speak so you do not forget them.$qb$, $qb$Ask to pause the discussion until both of you are calmer.$qb$, $qb$Interrupt to correct the point you disagree with before the discussion moves on.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2359$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A senior person criticizes your analysis sharply and you notice yourself focusing on embarrassment rather than their evidence. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Pause, identify the specific analytical point, and address the evidence first; handle the delivery issue separately if needed.$qb$, $qb$Implement a practical interim adjustment to address immediate operational needs while monitoring results.$qb$, $qb$Maintain existing procedures temporarily until formal company-wide guidance is published.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the issue to departmental leadership without first gathering supporting data or options.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the operational discrepancy and proceed with business as usual.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-243$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are presenting when a senior participant challenges your conclusion unexpectedly. You notice your heart rate rise and your speech speeding up. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Pause briefly, slow your delivery, ask or confirm the specific concern, and respond to that point rather than reacting to the challenge itself.$qb$, $qb$Continue presenting and answer the challenge after you finish your planned slides.$qb$, $qb$Respond immediately with the strongest evidence supporting your conclusion.$qb$, $qb$Tell the participant you would prefer questions at the end because interruptions make presenting difficult.$qb$, $qb$Rush through the remaining slides so the discussion can end sooner.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2589$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A senior leader says a project should continue because substantial money has already been spent. New evidence shows the expected benefit has fallen sharply. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess the decision from future costs, benefits and alternatives rather than allowing sunk cost to determine whether the project should continue.$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the conflicting evidence privately while supporting the senior leader's position in public.$qb$, $qb$Rely on historical impressions or positive sentiment and assume performance will normalize.$qb$, $qb$Challenge the prevailing opinion confrontational in open forums without structured evidence.$qb$, $qb$Ignore objective operational metrics and base decisions entirely on authority or sentiment.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2816$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague dismisses your suggestion in a sharp tone, and you notice yourself wanting to reject their next idea without considering it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Notice the reaction, assess their next idea on its evidence, and address the tone separately if it needs discussion.$qb$, $qb$Challenge their next idea more strongly so the exchange is balanced.$qb$, $qb$Stop contributing to the discussion.$qb$, $qb$Support their idea to prove you are not upset.$qb$, $qb$Ask another colleague to judge every idea from that person.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-289$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You receive praise for a piece of work and notice yourself becoming overconfident about a similar task that is not yet complete. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Notice the confidence shift, separate the completed success from the new task, and review the new work against its own evidence and checks before assuming the same result.$qb$, $qb$Use the successful approach again because it has now been validated.$qb$, $qb$Ask for fewer reviews on the new task because your recent work was strong.$qb$, $qb$Tell yourself not to become overconfident but otherwise continue as planned.$qb$, $qb$Take on extra scope in the new task while your confidence is high.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-381$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague praises your idea and immediately asks you to approve a related change. You notice the praise makes you more inclined to agree. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Notice the influence of the positive feedback, separate it from the change request, and evaluate the change against the same evidence and criteria you would otherwise use.$qb$, $qb$Approve the change if it is closely related to the idea that was praised.$qb$, $qb$Delay the decision until the positive feeling has completely passed.$qb$, $qb$Ask another colleague to decide because your judgment may now be biased.$qb$, $qb$Approve it to maintain the constructive relationship created by the praise.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-473$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You receive a message that appears dismissive of your work. You are about to reply immediately and notice that you are angry. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Pause before replying, separate the message's factual content from your emotional interpretation, and respond after clarifying intent if needed.$qb$, $qb$Write the reply immediately but save it as a draft until later.$qb$, $qb$Tell the sender their tone is unacceptable before discussing the work.$qb$, $qb$Forward the message to a colleague and ask whether they also find it dismissive.$qb$, $qb$Reply with equal directness so the sender understands how the message felt.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-611$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague receives credit for work you contributed to, and you notice resentment affecting how you respond to their next idea. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Notice the resentment, separate the recognition issue from the new idea, evaluate the idea fairly, and raise the credit concern separately if it needs correction.$qb$, $qb$Challenge the new idea more carefully so the colleague does not receive unearned credit again.$qb$, $qb$Stay silent on their idea until the recognition issue is resolved.$qb$, $qb$Tell the team immediately that you also contributed to the earlier work.$qb$, $qb$Support the idea publicly but complain about the colleague privately.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-657$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You make a small mistake in a meeting and notice embarrassment making you unusually quiet afterward. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Notice the withdrawal, correct the mistake if needed, and deliberately re-engage with the remaining discussion rather than allowing one error to determine your participation.$qb$, $qb$Stay quiet for the rest of the meeting so you do not make another mistake.$qb$, $qb$Explain the mistake at length so everyone understands why it happened.$qb$, $qb$Leave the meeting early if your contribution is no longer essential.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the next meeting to participate normally again.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-703$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You receive a compliment from a customer and notice yourself becoming less critical of another request they make immediately afterward. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Notice the positive influence, separate the compliment from the new request, and evaluate the request using the normal criteria and constraints.$qb$, $qb$Approve the request if it is small because maintaining goodwill matters.$qb$, $qb$Delay all decisions involving the customer until the positive feeling passes.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to decide because you are no longer objective.$qb$, $qb$Give the customer extra flexibility as recognition for their positive feedback.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-749$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A senior colleague agrees with your idea in a meeting, and you notice you become less willing to consider objections raised afterward. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Notice the status-based confidence boost, return to the objections on their evidence, and evaluate them as you would have before the senior colleague supported you.$qb$, $qb$Keep the idea because senior support confirms it is strong.$qb$, $qb$Ask the senior colleague to respond to the objections.$qb$, $qb$Delay the decision until you feel less influenced.$qb$, $qb$Defend the idea more firmly so the group does not reopen an agreed direction.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-795$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague interrupts you twice in a meeting and you notice irritation making you stop listening to their point. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Notice the irritation, return attention to what they are saying, address the interruption pattern calmly at an appropriate moment, and judge the content separately.$qb$, $qb$Interrupt them back so they understand the effect.$qb$, $qb$Ignore everything they say until they apologize.$qb$, $qb$Leave the issue until after the meeting but continue mentally preparing your response.$qb$, $qb$Ask the chair to stop them from speaking over you.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-841$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague questions your expertise in a way you find irritating. You notice you are about to respond by listing your credentials. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Notice the defensiveness, ask what specific part of the work they doubt, and respond to the evidence or issue rather than defending your status.$qb$, $qb$Explain your experience so the colleague understands why your view should be trusted.$qb$, $qb$End the discussion because the comment is disrespectful.$qb$, $qb$Ask a senior colleague to confirm your expertise.$qb$, $qb$Question the colleague's qualifications in return.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-933$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your suggestion receives no response in a meeting and you notice yourself assuming people dislike it.$qb$, $qb$Notice the assumption, ask whether clarification or concerns are needed, and judge the silence only after gathering feedback.$qb$, $qb$Withdraw the suggestion.$qb$, $qb$Repeat it more strongly.$qb$, $qb$Treat silence as approval.$qb$, $qb$Complain afterward.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-979$qb$, $qb$A1-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Unexpected criticism just before a presentation makes you focus on proving the critic wrong.$qb$, $qb$Notice the reaction, refocus on the audience and presentation objective, and assess the criticism separately later.$qb$, $qb$Change the presentation immediately.$qb$, $qb$Mention the criticism during the presentation.$qb$, $qb$Ignore all future feedback from that person.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to present.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1298$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your manager asks you to promise a customer a faster turnaround, but the operations owner says current capacity cannot reliably meet it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify the actual capacity and customer priority, then align on a realistic commitment or specific capacity change before making the promise.$qb$, $qb$Promise the faster turnaround because your manager requested it.$qb$, $qb$Keep the current turnaround and ignore the manager's request.$qb$, $qb$Ask operations to work overtime after the promise is made.$qb$, $qb$Tell the customer that internal teams disagree.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1344$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your manager wants a decision today, while one dependency can only be confirmed tomorrow. The decision is reversible at low cost. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Explain the dependency and reversibility, recommend a provisional decision with a clear review trigger tomorrow, and avoid unnecessary delay or false certainty.$qb$, $qb$Wait until tomorrow because all dependencies should be confirmed.$qb$, $qb$Make the decision today and never revisit it.$qb$, $qb$Ask the other team to guess the outcome.$qb$, $qb$Tell your manager to decide without your recommendation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1390$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your manager asks you to accelerate delivery by starting work before a customer requirement is finalized. Some early work is reusable whichever option the customer chooses. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify the work that is genuinely option-independent, start that portion, and hold decision-dependent work until the requirement is clear while making the boundary visible.$qb$, $qb$Start all work using the most likely requirement.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the full requirement before doing anything.$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer to choose immediately even if they are not ready.$qb$, $qb$Start the cheapest version and change it later.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1482$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your manager asks you to start a supplier change immediately, but operations says the new supplier's packaging has not yet been tested on existing equipment. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify any work that can start safely, keep equipment-dependent rollout conditional on the packaging test, and align both parties on the fastest controlled sequence.$qb$, $qb$Start the full change because your manager requested urgency.$qb$, $qb$Wait for every aspect of the supplier change before doing anything.$qb$, $qb$Ask operations to accept the packaging risk temporarily.$qb$, $qb$Switch suppliers and solve equipment issues if they appear.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1528$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your manager wants a customer demonstration tomorrow, but the technical owner says one feature is unstable. The rest of the demonstration is ready. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Agree a demonstration that excludes or clearly labels the unstable feature, show what is reliable, and align on when the feature can be demonstrated safely.$qb$, $qb$Demonstrate everything and hope the feature works.$qb$, $qb$Cancel the entire demonstration.$qb$, $qb$Ask the technical owner to accept responsibility if the feature fails.$qb$, $qb$Describe the unstable feature as working without showing it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1574$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your manager wants a proposal sent today, but one price assumption is still awaiting supplier confirmation. The proposal can clearly state a provisional price. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Send only if the provisional nature, assumption and confirmation point are explicit and commercially acceptable, then update the customer when the supplier confirms.$qb$, $qb$Use the expected price as final to meet the deadline.$qb$, $qb$Wait for supplier confirmation and miss today's request.$qb$, $qb$Ask the supplier to guarantee the expected price verbally.$qb$, $qb$Remove pricing from the proposal entirely.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-194$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your manager gives verbal instructions that differ from the written project plan approved last week. Other team members are still following the written plan. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check the change with your manager, confirm whether it supersedes the approved plan, and communicate the clarified direction to teammates before work diverges further.$qb$, $qb$Follow the verbal instruction yourself and tell teammates that the manager may be changing direction.$qb$, $qb$Continue with the written plan until the manager updates it formally.$qb$, $qb$Ask teammates which instruction they think should take priority.$qb$, $qb$Use whichever instruction is easier for your own tasks and let the manager reconcile differences later.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2356$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A manager needs a status note now. One figure is still being validated, but the meeting decision does not depend on it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Send the validated decision-relevant status on time, clearly mark the one figure as pending, and provide it when confirmed.$qb$, $qb$Send the full status note with an estimated figure and verify it after the meeting.$qb$, $qb$Delay sending the status note until all figures are 100% verified, missing the meeting deadline.$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to lead the meeting without any written status notes.$qb$, $qb$Send an incomplete draft without mentioning that figures are unvalidated or pending.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-240$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your manager asks for a detailed analysis by Friday, but on Wednesday adds several urgent operational tasks without discussing the Friday commitment. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Show the remaining analysis effort and the new operational workload, explain the likely impact on Friday, and ask the manager to confirm the priority or adjust the scope.$qb$, $qb$Complete the operational tasks first and tell the manager on Thursday that the analysis may be late.$qb$, $qb$Work longer hours so both commitments can remain unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Protect the Friday analysis and delay the new tasks because it was assigned first.$qb$, $qb$Ask teammates to absorb the operational tasks without involving the manager.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2814$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your manager asks you to finish a briefing today, but the legal reviewer says one statement must be checked before external use. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Complete the rest of the briefing, isolate the statement requiring review, and align with your manager on whether the external version waits or uses verified alternative wording.$qb$, $qb$Send the briefing with the statement marked 'to be confirmed.'$qb$, $qb$Remove the statement without checking whether it is essential.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the legal reviewer because the manager set today's deadline.$qb$, $qb$Ask legal to take ownership of the entire briefing.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-286$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your manager asks you to 'make the report more strategic' but gives no examples, and the report is due in two days. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask what decisions and strategic questions the report should support, confirm which detail can be reduced, and share a revised outline before completing the full report.$qb$, $qb$Add more commentary about long-term trends and send the finished report for feedback.$qb$, $qb$Look at reports prepared by senior colleagues and copy their style.$qb$, $qb$Ask a teammate what they think the manager means by strategic.$qb$, $qb$Keep the existing report because the instruction is too vague to apply reliably.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-470$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your manager asks you to finish a task today but also tells you not to postpone a scheduled customer commitment. You cannot do both with the available time. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Explain the time conflict and impact, offer realistic options for scope, support or timing, and ask the manager to confirm the trade-off rather than silently choosing one commitment.$qb$, $qb$Complete the customer commitment first because external commitments are more important.$qb$, $qb$Finish the manager's task first because it was a direct instruction.$qb$, $qb$Work late so both commitments can remain unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to cover the customer commitment without checking with your manager.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-516$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your manager asks you to support another team for two days, but your current project lead expects you full-time and does not know about the request. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell your manager about the existing project commitment, clarify the impact of two days away, and align with the project lead on priorities and coverage before switching work.$qb$, $qb$Support the other team because your direct manager requested it.$qb$, $qb$Tell the project lead you will be unavailable for two days and let them raise any objection.$qb$, $qb$Try to do both roles during the two days.$qb$, $qb$Ask the other team to wait until your project is less busy.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-562$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your manager asks for a quick answer today, while a senior specialist says the issue needs more analysis before any recommendation is safe. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell your manager what can be answered confidently today, explain the specialist's unresolved risk, and agree whether a provisional answer or additional analysis best serves the decision.$qb$, $qb$Give the manager your best answer today and mention that more analysis may follow.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the specialist's full analysis and miss the manager's requested timing.$qb$, $qb$Ask the specialist to speak directly with your manager.$qb$, $qb$Give no answer because specialist caution should override management urgency.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-608$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your manager asks you to shorten a report significantly, while the report owner in another department says all current sections are required. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify the decision need behind your manager's request and the requirement behind each section, then align the two owners on what can be reduced without removing necessary content.$qb$, $qb$Shorten the report because your manager gave the latest instruction.$qb$, $qb$Keep every section because the report owner defines the format.$qb$, $qb$Create a short version for your manager and a full version for the other department every time.$qb$, $qb$Ask the two leaders to resolve the disagreement without providing any analysis.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-700$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your manager tells you to involve a specialist before making a decision. The specialist says the issue is routine and you should decide it yourself. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify with the specialist what input is actually needed, document or obtain that input, and ensure your manager's requirement is satisfied without transferring the whole decision unnecessarily.$qb$, $qb$Make the decision yourself because the specialist says it is routine.$qb$, $qb$Ask the specialist to make the decision so your manager's instruction is followed.$qb$, $qb$Tell your manager the specialist refused to get involved.$qb$, $qb$Delay the decision until the specialist agrees to participate fully.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-746$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your manager asks you to give a stakeholder an answer today, but the policy owner says the relevant rule is being clarified and may change tomorrow. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell your manager what can be stated safely today, explain the pending policy clarification, and align on a provisional response that does not create a commitment the final rule may contradict.$qb$, $qb$Give the stakeholder the current rule and update them tomorrow if it changes.$qb$, $qb$Wait until tomorrow and provide no answer today.$qb$, $qb$Ask the policy owner to speak directly to the stakeholder.$qb$, $qb$Give your best interpretation so the manager's deadline is met.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-792$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your manager asks you to prepare an urgent briefing, but a project sponsor has already committed your full afternoon to a workshop. Neither knows about the other's request. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Make the conflict visible immediately, explain the impact of each commitment, and align the manager and sponsor on priority, coverage or a revised scope before promising both.$qb$, $qb$Do the manager's briefing because direct-manager requests take priority.$qb$, $qb$Attend the workshop because it was committed first.$qb$, $qb$Try to prepare the briefing during the workshop.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to attend the workshop in your place without consulting the sponsor.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-884$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your manager asks you to decide quickly, but another manager who owns a key dependency asks you not to proceed until their team reviews it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Make the dependency and timing conflict explicit, clarify what review is genuinely required, and align the managers on a path protecting both urgency and dependency risk.$qb$, $qb$Follow your own manager's instruction.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the other team regardless of urgency.$qb$, $qb$Ask the managers to resolve it without your involvement.$qb$, $qb$Proceed and tell the dependency team afterward.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-976$qb$, $qb$A1-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your manager wants an early draft today, but one section depends on data that will arrive tomorrow. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Draft what can be supported today, mark the data-dependent section clearly, and align expectations for completing it tomorrow.$qb$, $qb$Use estimates for the missing section.$qb$, $qb$Send nothing until tomorrow.$qb$, $qb$Ask the data owner to write the whole draft.$qb$, $qb$Remove the section.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-013$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are transferred to a new team that has established informal norms and communication habits different from your previous department. How do you integrate effectively?$qb$, $qb$Observe team interactions during the first few weeks, adopt established communication preferences, and build rapport through reliable work delivery.$qb$, $qb$Propose adopting a few proven communication templates from your old department to improve the team's current efficiency.$qb$, $qb$Focus on delivering your assigned tasks independently while participating politely in formal team meetings.$qb$, $qb$Schedule 1-on-1 meetings with senior team members to ask for advice on how to stand out within the team.$qb$, $qb$Maintain your previous working style, assuming team performance depends on individual deliverable quality rather than team norms.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-059$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your project team is working hard, but two members keep making related decisions separately and the outputs no longer fit together. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Bring the related decisions together, clarify who owns what, define consultation points, and agree how future cross-impact decisions will be coordinated.$qb$, $qb$Ask both members to copy you on future messages so you can watch for conflicts.$qb$, $qb$Choose one person's approach and ask the other to align with it.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to resolve the differences themselves before the next meeting.$qb$, $qb$Ask the project manager to approve every decision in both areas.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-105$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$One project member is highly capable but rarely shares progress until work is complete. This creates surprises for others who depend on their work. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Agree on meaningful progress and dependency updates, explain why visibility matters, and preserve their autonomy while preventing late surprises.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to send you a daily status email.$qb$, $qb$Require them to attend every project meeting.$qb$, $qb$Move dependent work away from them.$qb$, $qb$Accept the style because their final work is strong.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1115$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team member changes the order of their tasks to work more efficiently, but the change causes another teammate to wait for an input they expected earlier. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify the dependency, agree which intermediate outputs have timing commitments, and let individuals optimize their sequence within those shared constraints.$qb$, $qb$Tell the team member never to change task order.$qb$, $qb$Ask the waiting teammate to plan more flexibly.$qb$, $qb$Require the project manager to approve every sequencing change.$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because the final deadline was not missed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1161$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate regularly finishes their own work early but then changes shared files to 'help' other workstreams without telling the owners. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the helpful intent, clarify ownership boundaries, and agree that cross-workstream changes are proposed or coordinated with the responsible owner before editing shared work.$qb$, $qb$Tell them never to help other workstreams.$qb$, $qb$Allow the changes because they usually improve the work.$qb$, $qb$Give them formal ownership of every file they edit.$qb$, $qb$Ask other teams to check version history for unexpected changes.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1253$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate frequently completes shared tasks without updating the status board, so others keep asking whether work is ready. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Agree that updating the shared status is part of completing dependent work, simplify the update if needed, and make handoff readiness visible without repeated personal checking.$qb$, $qb$Ask everyone to message the teammate when they need status.$qb$, $qb$Update the board for them whenever you notice completion.$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because the actual work is finished.$qb$, $qb$Require hourly status updates from the whole team.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1483$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team member updates a customer document directly but forgets to tell the colleague who is preparing training from the same content. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify which content changes require cross-team notification, update the training owner now, and establish a shared change signal for dependent materials.$qb$, $qb$Tell the training colleague to check the customer document daily.$qb$, $qb$Require approval before any customer document can change.$qb$, $qb$Leave it because the customer document is the official source.$qb$, $qb$Ask the team member to maintain the training material too.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-149$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You join a project team with an unfamiliar meeting routine that seems inefficient to you. You do not yet understand why the team uses it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Participate, ask what purpose the routine serves, observe its effect on delivery, and suggest a change only after you understand the context.$qb$, $qb$Follow the routine for a few meetings and then propose the method that worked in your previous team.$qb$, $qb$Tell the project lead privately that the routine appears inefficient and ask whether it can be changed.$qb$, $qb$Avoid suggesting any change because a new member should adapt to established practices.$qb$, $qb$Stop attending the parts that appear irrelevant to your own tasks.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1529$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate completes their work on time but changes a shared deadline without telling the person whose task follows it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Correct the dependency dates together and make notification of downstream-impacting changes part of the shared planning routine.$qb$, $qb$Tell the next person to monitor the plan more closely.$qb$, $qb$Require manager approval for every deadline change.$qb$, $qb$Leave it because the shared plan shows the new date.$qb$, $qb$Ask the project manager to make all date changes.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1575$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate changes the format of a shared data file to make their own work easier, but another team's automated import then fails. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Restore the compatible format, clarify which shared-file elements are interface requirements, and agree how local improvements can be made without breaking downstream use.$qb$, $qb$Tell the other team to update its import.$qb$, $qb$Ban all format changes.$qb$, $qb$Keep the new format because it is easier for the producing team.$qb$, $qb$Ask both teams to maintain separate versions.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-195$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Two experienced members of a new project team dominate discussions, while other members mostly wait for instructions. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Structure discussions so different members prepare or lead relevant sections, invite quieter members' analysis before decisions close, and clarify shared ownership of work.$qb$, $qb$Ask the two experienced members to leave more time for others to speak.$qb$, $qb$Invite quieter members to send ideas to you privately after meetings.$qb$, $qb$Let the experienced members lead because the project will move faster.$qb$, $qb$Assign quieter members tasks after meetings without involving them in decisions.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-241$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A new team member rarely contributes because most project decisions are discussed informally between long-standing colleagues before meetings. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Bring material decisions into the shared team forum, provide context before meetings, and make sure the new member can contribute before decisions are effectively closed.$qb$, $qb$Tell the new member which colleagues they should speak with informally before meetings.$qb$, $qb$Invite the new member to join the informal discussions whenever you notice one happening.$qb$, $qb$Let the pattern continue until the new member builds stronger relationships.$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to assign the new member simpler tasks that need fewer informal decisions.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-287$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project team has divided into two informal groups that rarely ask each other for input. Work is still progressing, but duplicate effort is beginning to appear. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Make overlapping work visible, create shared checkpoints around common decisions, and pair members across the two groups where their work depends on each other.$qb$, $qb$Tell both groups that they need to communicate more often.$qb$, $qb$Ask the project manager to merge the two groups formally.$qb$, $qb$Let the groups continue because informal working relationships are normal.$qb$, $qb$Choose one group as the main source of decisions to reduce duplication.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-333$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project team has one highly experienced member who answers most questions before others have time to think. Less-experienced members are becoming dependent on them. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask the experienced member to give others space to propose answers first, rotate ownership of suitable problems, and use their expertise to coach rather than immediately solve.$qb$, $qb$Ask less-experienced members to speak faster when they know an answer.$qb$, $qb$Keep the current approach because quick expert answers help the project.$qb$, $qb$Stop inviting the experienced member to routine discussions.$qb$, $qb$Assign all difficult problems to the experienced member and simple ones to everyone else.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-379$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A new team has members with strong specialist expertise, but people keep solving only the parts related to their own discipline. Cross-cutting issues remain unresolved. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Make the cross-cutting issues explicit, assign mixed ownership around shared outcomes, and use specialist input as contributions to one team decision rather than separate answers.$qb$, $qb$Ask each specialist to provide a written recommendation for their part.$qb$, $qb$Give cross-cutting issues to the project manager because no single specialist owns them.$qb$, $qb$Let specialists solve their own areas and combine the outputs at the end.$qb$, $qb$Assign each cross-cutting issue to whichever specialist has the most seniority.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-517$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team has strong individual performers, but meetings often end without anyone connecting how one person's decision affects another person's work. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Add a brief dependency check before decisions close, ask affected members to confirm impacts, and record cross-owner actions so individual decisions become coordinated team execution.$qb$, $qb$Ask everyone to send more detailed updates before meetings.$qb$, $qb$Let each owner manage downstream impacts after the meeting.$qb$, $qb$Ask the project manager to review all decisions for cross-team impact.$qb$, $qb$Create more meetings so each workstream can discuss dependencies separately.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-563$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project team has agreed roles, but people still duplicate work because they interpret the role boundaries differently. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use recent overlaps to clarify where each role starts and ends, document shared interfaces, and agree how work crossing boundaries will be coordinated.$qb$, $qb$Remind everyone of the existing role titles and ask them to stay within them.$qb$, $qb$Let duplication continue temporarily because extra coverage reduces risk.$qb$, $qb$Ask the project manager to approve every task assignment.$qb$, $qb$Assign each duplicated task permanently to the person who completed it fastest.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-747$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project team has several good ideas, but each person keeps developing their own solution and little is being combined. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Create a shared comparison of the ideas against the project outcome and constraints, combine complementary elements where useful, and assign one integrated path rather than parallel personal solutions.$qb$, $qb$Ask everyone to finish their idea and choose the best one later.$qb$, $qb$Select the idea from the most experienced team member.$qb$, $qb$Let people continue independently because competition may produce better ideas.$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to choose one solution immediately.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-793$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team has agreed a shared deadline, but members optimize their own tasks without noticing that one person's late intermediate output blocks three others. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Map the critical handoffs, make intermediate dependencies visible, and agree earlier checkpoints for outputs that unlock other people's work.$qb$, $qb$Remind everyone that the final deadline is shared.$qb$, $qb$Ask the blocking person to finish all their work earlier.$qb$, $qb$Add daily status meetings for the whole team.$qb$, $qb$Let each member manage their own schedule because they know their tasks best.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-885$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Two team members need the same specialist during the same week, but neither knew about the other's dependency. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Make the shared dependency visible, coordinate the timing and priorities, and include scarce shared resources in future team planning.$qb$, $qb$Let the specialist choose which request comes first.$qb$, $qb$Ask both members to find alternatives independently.$qb$, $qb$Prioritize the task with the earlier deadline automatically.$qb$, $qb$Escalate every shared-resource conflict to the manager.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-931$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate completes work using assumptions that changed yesterday in another workstream.$qb$, $qb$Align the affected members on current assumptions, assess rework, and establish a shared assumption-update point.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to redo everything.$qb$, $qb$Let each workstream keep its own assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Ask the project manager to reconcile weekly.$qb$, $qb$Keep the work because it was correct when started.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-977$qb$, $qb$A1-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate helps others so often that their own critical handoffs are late.$qb$, $qb$Discuss the pattern, clarify dependency priorities, and agree how they can help without silently sacrificing their own critical commitments.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to stop helping others.$qb$, $qb$Accept the delays because they are collaborative.$qb$, $qb$Tell others not to ask them for help.$qb$, $qb$Move all their deadlines earlier.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-016$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your department introduces an AI summary tool to assist with analyzing client feedback surveys. How do you incorporate this technology into your daily reporting workflow?$qb$, $qb$Use the AI tool to draft initial theme categorizations, thoroughly verify the outputs against raw client responses, and refine final insights.$qb$, $qb$Copy the AI-generated survey summaries directly into final client reports to save operational processing time.$qb$, $qb$Manually analyze all client feedback surveys as usual, using the AI tool only as a secondary check after completing your report.$qb$, $qb$Forward raw client feedback files into the AI tool and distribute generated summaries directly to department leadership.$qb$, $qb$Decline to use the AI tool until long-term accuracy benchmarks are published by the IT department.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-062$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI tool summarizes hundreds of service comments and identifies a new complaint theme. A quick sample suggests some comments may have been classified incorrectly. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Validate the theme against a representative sample, inspect likely misclassifications, and use the AI output only after judging whether the pattern is real.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI tool to rerun the analysis with a different prompt.$qb$, $qb$Use the identified theme but label it as AI-generated in the report.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the new theme because the tool made some classification mistakes.$qb$, $qb$Accept the result because manually reviewing hundreds of comments would take too long.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-108$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant drafts a recommendation that appears strong, but it cites two facts you cannot find in the approved source material. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Verify the claims independently, remove or correct unsupported facts, and reassess whether the recommendation still holds before using it.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to provide citations and accept them if they look credible.$qb$, $qb$Delete the two facts but keep the recommendation unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Use the draft with a note that it was AI-assisted.$qb$, $qb$Reject the entire AI draft and start again manually.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1118$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant generates a list of overdue accounts, but you notice one account marked overdue actually has a payment arrangement recorded in another system. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check whether the AI has access to payment arrangements, validate affected accounts against the authoritative source, and adjust the workflow before acting on the overdue list.$qb$, $qb$Remove that one account and use the rest of the list.$qb$, $qb$Contact every account because the AI probably identified genuine risk.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI whether each account has a payment arrangement and trust its answer.$qb$, $qb$Stop using the AI list entirely.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1164$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant drafts a calculation formula that works on your sample but rounds intermediate values differently from the finance rule. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check the required rounding rule, test cases where the difference matters, and correct the formula before using it in financial outputs.$qb$, $qb$Use the formula because the sample result matches.$qb$, $qb$Round only the final answer manually.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI whether its rounding is acceptable.$qb$, $qb$Use the formula and fix any complaints later.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1256$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant extracts action items from meeting notes but assigns one action to the wrong person because two attendees have similar names. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Verify owners against the meeting context, correct the action, and check other similar-name assignments before publishing the action list.$qb$, $qb$Correct that one name and publish the rest immediately.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to regenerate the list and trust the new version.$qb$, $qb$Send the list and let attendees correct their own actions.$qb$, $qb$Stop using AI for meeting notes.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1486$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant drafts a list of customers eligible for a benefit but includes one customer whose eligibility expired yesterday. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check how the AI handles effective dates, validate eligibility against the current authoritative record, and review other time-sensitive cases before using the list.$qb$, $qb$Remove that customer and use the rest of the list.$qb$, $qb$Give the benefit because the customer was eligible recently.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to rerun today's list and trust it.$qb$, $qb$Stop using AI for eligibility work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-152$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI tool produces a convincing summary of a policy document, but one statement conflicts with what you remember. You need the summary for a meeting this afternoon. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check the disputed statement against the authoritative policy, verify other decision-critical points, correct the summary, and use it only after that review.$qb$, $qb$Check the one statement that looks wrong and use the rest of the summary if it is corrected.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to review its own summary and use the revised version if it appears consistent.$qb$, $qb$Use the summary but tell the meeting that it was AI-generated and may contain errors.$qb$, $qb$Discard the summary and rely on your memory because the AI has shown it cannot be trusted.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1578$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant summarizes a set of orders and counts cancelled orders as completed because both have a closed status. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check the status definitions, separate cancellation from successful completion, correct the measure, and validate other closed-status categories before reporting.$qb$, $qb$Remove the cancelled orders manually and use the rest.$qb$, $qb$Count them as completed because they are closed.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to calculate again and trust the result.$qb$, $qb$Stop using AI for operational summaries.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-198$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant produces a spreadsheet formula that works on your sample data. The formula will be used in a high-volume monthly report. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Test it on representative cases and edge conditions, compare results with a trusted calculation, and review the formula logic before production use.$qb$, $qb$Test it on several more sample rows and use it if those results are correct.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to explain the formula and use it if the explanation sounds reasonable.$qb$, $qb$Use it in the report but spot-check a few outputs afterward.$qb$, $qb$Use it because the sample worked and replace it later if an error appears.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2842$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant creates a summary of customer complaints but combines two different products with similar names. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Separate the products using reliable identifiers, recheck the affected complaints and conclusions, and validate the corrected summary before using it.$qb$, $qb$Manually rename the products in the summary without checking the source complaints.$qb$, $qb$Use the summary because the products are similar.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to regenerate the summary with a warning not to confuse them.$qb$, $qb$Discard all AI analysis and read every complaint manually.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-290$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant summarizes a long technical document accurately in most places but omits one limitation that could affect a decision. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Verify the decision-critical sections against the source, restore the omitted limitation, and use the summary only with the material caveat included.$qb$, $qb$Add the limitation you noticed and use the rest of the summary as written.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to produce a longer summary so omissions are less likely.$qb$, $qb$Attach the full technical document and use the AI summary unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Stop using AI summaries because omissions make them unsafe.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-336$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI tool generates a customer segmentation that looks useful, but you cannot tell which variables drove several surprising groupings. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Investigate the inputs and grouping logic or validation evidence, test whether the surprising segments are meaningful, and avoid using them for decisions until the result can be reasonably explained and checked.$qb$, $qb$Use the segments but monitor whether campaigns perform differently than expected.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to describe why it created each group and accept the explanation.$qb$, $qb$Remove the surprising customers from the analysis and use the remaining segments.$qb$, $qb$Use the output because unexpected patterns are often where AI adds the most value.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-428$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant suggests a data-cleaning rule that removes many unusual records. The resulting chart looks much cleaner. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Examine why the records are unusual, test whether they are errors or valid edge cases, and apply the rule only if removing them is justified for the analysis.$qb$, $qb$Use the rule because cleaner data usually produces more reliable charts.$qb$, $qb$Remove only half of the unusual records to reduce the risk of over-cleaning.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI whether the records are likely to be errors and follow its answer.$qb$, $qb$Keep all records because AI should never decide which data is valid.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-474$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI tool suggests that a customer is likely to leave, but the prediction conflicts with recent positive conversations. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the factors and data behind the prediction where possible, compare them with current customer evidence, and use both as inputs rather than treating either the model or recent conversation as decisive alone.$qb$, $qb$Trust the model because it uses more historical data than one conversation.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the model because recent direct customer feedback is more current.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to recalculate the prediction using the latest conversation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the customer immediately as high risk without further checking.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-520$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant creates a chart with a dramatic trend, but you notice the vertical axis starts well above zero. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check whether the axis choice is appropriate for the measure and audience, review the underlying data, and adjust or clearly explain the scale so the visual does not exaggerate the change.$qb$, $qb$Keep the chart because the underlying numbers are correct.$qb$, $qb$Reset every chart axis to zero because that is always more honest.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI to choose a more neutral chart style.$qb$, $qb$Remove the chart and present only the raw numbers.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-658$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant proposes a formula that gives the right answer for positive values but you are unsure how it behaves with blanks or negative values. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Test blanks, negatives, boundary values and representative cases, inspect the formula logic, and use it only after its behavior matches the business rule.$qb$, $qb$Use it because the normal production values are usually positive.$qb$, $qb$Add a condition that converts all negatives to zero.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI whether it handles blanks and negatives correctly.$qb$, $qb$Use it and correct unusual results if they appear later.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-750$qb$, $qb$A1-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An AI assistant generates a customer email containing a statistic that looks reasonable but does not cite where it came from. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Verify the statistic against an authoritative source, correct or remove it if unsupported, and check other factual claims before sending the AI-assisted message.$qb$, $qb$Keep the statistic because it is plausible and not central to the email.$qb$, $qb$Ask the AI where the statistic came from and use its answer.$qb$, $qb$Remove only the statistic and send the rest without review.$qb$, $qb$Add 'approximately' before the statistic so exact sourcing matters less.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1162$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Employees manually rename downloaded files using a standard customer ID before uploading them to another system. Naming errors are common. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the required identifier and test an approved automated naming or upload step that pulls the ID from the source, with exception handling for unusual cases.$qb$, $qb$Give employees a naming checklist.$qb$, $qb$Ask a second person to verify every filename.$qb$, $qb$Tell employees to copy and paste the ID more carefully.$qb$, $qb$Accept occasional naming errors because the systems are separate.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1208$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Employees manually compare delivery addresses against a reference list even though the system can validate postal codes automatically.$qb$, $qb$Test the approved validation capability, define exceptions requiring human review, and remove repetitive manual comparison where the system reliably covers the rule.$qb$, $qb$Keep manual checking as a second control for every address.$qb$, $qb$Tell employees to work faster.$qb$, $qb$Stop all address checking.$qb$, $qb$Assign one specialist to check every address.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1254$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team manually copies approved product descriptions into several standard documents, and wording sometimes becomes inconsistent. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify the authoritative description source and test approved reusable fields or document generation so standard wording is inserted consistently, with human review for exceptions.$qb$, $qb$Ask staff to copy more carefully.$qb$, $qb$Create a checklist comparing every document.$qb$, $qb$Assign one person to maintain all documents manually.$qb$, $qb$Allow small wording differences because the meaning is similar.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1300$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team manually re-enters customer contact details from an approved online form into a second internal system. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the required fields and data controls, then explore an approved integration or import that transfers the data once, with exception handling for records that fail validation.$qb$, $qb$Give staff a faster data-entry template.$qb$, $qb$Ask customers to submit the information twice.$qb$, $qb$Add a second checker to catch typing errors.$qb$, $qb$Accept manual entry because the systems have different purposes.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1346$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Employees manually copy approved pricing text into customer quotes, and small wording differences keep appearing.$qb$, $qb$Use one governed pricing-text source or approved reusable field, validate the generated wording, and reserve manual editing for genuine exceptions.$qb$, $qb$Tell employees to copy more carefully.$qb$, $qb$Add a second reviewer to every quote.$qb$, $qb$Let each team maintain its own approved wording.$qb$, $qb$Accept minor wording differences if prices are correct.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1392$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Staff manually sort incoming requests into folders using a simple rule based on request type, and misfiling causes delays. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the routing rules and test approved automatic classification or form-based routing, with human review for ambiguous requests.$qb$, $qb$Create a sorting checklist beside each workstation.$qb$, $qb$Ask a second person to check each folder.$qb$, $qb$Tell requesters to put the folder name in the subject line.$qb$, $qb$Accept some misfiling because manual sorting is flexible.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1484$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Employees manually download a daily report, rename it, and upload it unchanged to a shared folder. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the destination and naming rules, then test an approved scheduled export or automated transfer with monitoring for failed runs.$qb$, $qb$Create a detailed checklist for the download and upload.$qb$, $qb$Rotate the task among employees.$qb$, $qb$Ask employees to upload it only twice a week.$qb$, $qb$Keep the manual process because it provides human oversight.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-150$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Employees repeatedly complete an old internal request form incorrectly, creating avoidable rework for your team. The form has been unchanged for years. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the common errors with users, identify which wording or steps create confusion, test a simpler version with a small group, and measure whether rework falls.$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the instructions in clearer language and ask the process owner to replace the current guidance.$qb$, $qb$Create a correctly completed example and send it whenever someone makes an error.$qb$, $qb$Ask managers to remind employees to read the existing instructions carefully.$qb$, $qb$Keep correcting the forms because changing an established process may create different problems.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-196$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A recurring internal approval takes five email exchanges even though most requests are routine and low risk. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Map why each exchange exists, separate routine from higher-risk requests, and test a simpler controlled path for routine cases with the process owner.$qb$, $qb$Create a standard email template so the five exchanges happen faster.$qb$, $qb$Ask approvers to respond within a shorter time target.$qb$, $qb$Remove two email steps for your own team's routine requests.$qb$, $qb$Accept the process because approvals are meant to be cautious.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-242$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Users abandon an internal workflow at the same step every week, and staff manually complete the step for them. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Observe the failure point, identify why users cannot complete it, test a simpler step or interface, and measure whether manual intervention falls.$qb$, $qb$Write clearer instructions for the difficult step and send them to users.$qb$, $qb$Create a standard staff workaround so manual completion is faster.$qb$, $qb$Ask users to attend a short training session before using the workflow.$qb$, $qb$Keep the manual support because it reliably gets the work completed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-288$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer-facing process requires staff to copy the same information manually into two systems. The duplication causes delays but both systems are currently required. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm why both records are required, explore an approved integration or single-entry handoff, and test a controlled way to remove duplicate entry without losing either system's required data.$qb$, $qb$Create a copy-and-paste template so staff can enter the information faster.$qb$, $qb$Ask staff to complete one system immediately and the other at the end of the day.$qb$, $qb$Stop entering the information in the less-used system until someone complains.$qb$, $qb$Accept the duplication because both systems are mandatory.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-334$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Employees repeatedly download a spreadsheet, edit it, and email it back for a routine request. Version confusion causes rework. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Map the information and approvals actually needed, test a shared controlled form or workflow, and measure whether version errors and handling time fall.$qb$, $qb$Create a stricter file-naming convention for returned spreadsheets.$qb$, $qb$Ask employees to copy one central mailbox on every version.$qb$, $qb$Have one coordinator manually track which spreadsheet is current.$qb$, $qb$Keep the process because spreadsheets are familiar to everyone.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-380$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Employees frequently ask support to look up information that already exists in three different internal systems. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify the information employees actually need, test a single searchable view or guided access point, and measure whether support requests fall without weakening data controls.$qb$, $qb$Create a document listing which system contains each type of information.$qb$, $qb$Train employees to search all three systems more effectively.$qb$, $qb$Ask support to create standard replies with links to the relevant systems.$qb$, $qb$Keep the current approach because each system has a different purpose.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-426$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Employees repeatedly call a support desk to ask whether their request has been approved, even though the status exists in an internal system. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Find out why employees cannot easily see or trust the status, test a clearer self-service status view or notification, and measure whether status calls fall.$qb$, $qb$Send employees instructions showing where the status appears.$qb$, $qb$Ask support staff to use a standard reply for status calls.$qb$, $qb$Add more staff during periods with many status calls.$qb$, $qb$Tell employees that support cannot answer questions already covered by the system.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-472$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An internal approval form asks users for information that the company already holds in another system. Users often enter it incorrectly. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the authoritative source and data rules, explore pre-filling or integrating the existing information, and test whether removing re-entry reduces errors without weakening control.$qb$, $qb$Add examples beside each field so users copy the information correctly.$qb$, $qb$Ask users to check the other system before completing the form.$qb$, $qb$Have staff correct the information after submission.$qb$, $qb$Keep the fields because asking users to confirm data provides an extra check.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-518$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A routine request is approved by email, then manually copied into a tracker, then manually copied again into another team's queue. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Map the required decision and records, identify where the same information is being re-entered, and test an approved handoff or integration that creates each required record from one source.$qb$, $qb$Create templates so the copying takes less time.$qb$, $qb$Assign one coordinator to perform all copying consistently.$qb$, $qb$Remove the tracker entry because the final queue is what matters.$qb$, $qb$Keep the process because each system belongs to a different team.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-564$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team manually checks every low-value request for the same three conditions, and almost all requests pass. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the risks those checks control, analyze exception patterns, and test automated validation or risk-based review so human attention is focused where judgment is needed.$qb$, $qb$Reduce the check to one person instead of two.$qb$, $qb$Sample only half the requests without changing the process formally.$qb$, $qb$Keep checking everything because the high pass rate proves the control works.$qb$, $qb$Stop the checks because almost all requests pass.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-610$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team spends time manually checking whether requests meet simple eligibility rules before sending them to a specialist. Most rejected requests fail the same two rules. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Move the two clear rules earlier into the request experience through validation or guidance, then measure whether specialist screening effort and rejected requests fall.$qb$, $qb$Give the checking team a faster checklist for the two common rules.$qb$, $qb$Ask specialists to reject ineligible requests themselves.$qb$, $qb$Train requesters on the eligibility rules every quarter.$qb$, $qb$Keep manual checking because it prevents specialists receiving bad requests.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-656$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A routine customer request requires staff to open four screens only to copy one identifier from each. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm which identifiers are actually needed, explore a consolidated view or automated lookup, and test whether the request can be completed with fewer manual navigation steps.$qb$, $qb$Create a written guide showing the fastest order for opening the four screens.$qb$, $qb$Ask staff to keep all four screens open throughout the day.$qb$, $qb$Assign the copying to a junior employee so specialists save time.$qb$, $qb$Keep the process because each screen belongs to a different system.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-702$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team manually sends the same status email to every requester even though the status is already updated in a central system. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Find out why requesters still need the email, improve notification or visibility from the central system, and test whether manual status messages can be removed safely.$qb$, $qb$Create an email template so staff send the updates faster.$qb$, $qb$Stop the emails immediately because the status is already available.$qb$, $qb$Ask requesters to check the system more often.$qb$, $qb$Assign one person to send all status emails in batches.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-748$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A support team manually copies common customer questions into a weekly report so another team can identify recurring issues. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Define the recurring information needed, explore structured tagging or automated aggregation at the point of support, and test whether the report can be generated without repeated manual copying.$qb$, $qb$Create a standard copy-and-paste template for the weekly report.$qb$, $qb$Ask support to send only the most important questions.$qb$, $qb$Assign one analyst to prepare the report more efficiently.$qb$, $qb$Keep the process because manual review helps people understand the questions.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-794$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Staff repeatedly copy a customer reference number from one system into another, and typing errors cause cases to be mislinked. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the systems' identifiers and controls, then test an approved lookup, scan, or integration that transfers the reference without manual retyping.$qb$, $qb$Add a second person to check every typed reference.$qb$, $qb$Ask staff to copy and paste instead of typing.$qb$, $qb$Add a warning reminding staff to check the number carefully.$qb$, $qb$Accept occasional errors because the systems cannot be changed quickly.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-840$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team manually sends reminders for documents that the system already knows are overdue. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the reminder rules and recipients, test automated notifications or task prompts, and retain manual intervention only for exceptions requiring judgment.$qb$, $qb$Create reminder templates so manual messages are faster.$qb$, $qb$Assign one person to send reminders each morning.$qb$, $qb$Stop reminders and expect users to check the system.$qb$, $qb$Send more frequent reminders so fewer items become very late.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-932$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Visitors  at a busy office sign a paper register and reception later retypes the same data into a security system.  The duplicate step creates queues and transcription errors. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Explore an approved digital check-in or integration that captures required data once and preserves security controls.$qb$, $qb$Give reception a faster typing template.$qb$, $qb$Add another receptionist.$qb$, $qb$Ask visitors to type into both systems.$qb$, $qb$Keep the process because both records are needed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-978$qb$, $qb$A1-08$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Staff copy dashboard figures into the same presentation every morning.$qb$, $qb$Explore an approved automated link or generated report, validate the result, and keep manual handling for exceptions.$qb$, $qb$Create a copy-paste checklist.$qb$, $qb$Assign one person to do it.$qb$, $qb$Update less often.$qb$, $qb$Keep manual copying because it is familiar.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-011$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your company shifts to a new CRM software platform, replacing the system you have used for three years. Some team members complain about the learning curve. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Embrace the new platform proactively, master key daily workflows, create quick-reference cheat sheets for peers, and share tips during team check-ins.$qb$, $qb$Attend all official training sessions carefully and adapt your daily work routines to match the new software requirements.$qb$, $qb$Continue using personal offline spreadsheets for tracking work while gradually transitioning entries into the new CRM.$qb$, $qb$Suggest to your supervisor that the department delay full migration until peak operational season passes.$qb$, $qb$Limit your use of the new CRM to mandatory fields only, maintaining legacy habits where possible.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-057$qb$, $qb$A1-09$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A new approval workflow is mandatory. It improves control but initially adds time to routine work, and colleagues are creating informal shortcuts. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use the new workflow, identify where the added time is necessary versus avoidable, and raise evidence-based improvements through the approved channel.$qb$, $qb$Follow the workflow yourself and let others decide how strictly they use it.$qb$, $qb$Use the shortcut for low-risk work until the official process becomes faster.$qb$, $qb$Return to the old workflow for urgent cases.$qb$, $qb$Tell colleagues the new process is inefficient and wait for management to redesign it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
